var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#Sampler-Setup","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"This guide explains the pieces needed to define a sampler interface with QUBODrivers. The smallest useful wrapper has two parts:\n\na QUBODrivers.@setup macro call that declares the optimizer type and attributes;\na QUBODrivers.sample method that reads the internal model, calls the backend, and returns a QUBOTools.SampleSet.","title":"Sampler Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#Imports","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"Import QUBODrivers, MathOptInterface, and QUBOTools. QUBOTools is available through the QUBODrivers module and provides model conversion, objective evaluation, sample containers, and metadata helpers.\n\nimport QUBODrivers\nimport QUBODrivers: QUBOTools\nimport MathOptInterface as MOI","title":"Imports"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#The-[QUBODrivers.@setup](@ref)-macro","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"This macro usually takes two arguments: the identifier of the sampler's struct (usually Optimizer) and a begin ... end block containing configuration parameters as key-value pairs.\n\nThe first parameter of the configuration block is the sampler's name, which will be used to identify it in the MOI.SolverName attribute.\n\nThe next entry is the version assignment, which is accessed by the MOI.SolverVersion attribute. In order to consistently support semantic versioning it is required that the version number comes as a v-string e.g. v\"major.minor.patch\".\n\nnote: Note\nIf missing, the version parameter matches the current version of QUBODrivers.jl.\n\nA simple yet valid @setup call would look like this:\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name    = \"Super Sampler\"\n    version = v\"1.0.2\"\nend\n\nThe generated optimizer has storage for the current QUBOTools model, raw attributes, the original MOI variable order, and fixed-variable metadata. If a sampler needs additional fields, define the optimizer type manually and implement the same methods described in the API Reference.","title":"The QUBODrivers.@setup macro"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#Attributes","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"The attributes parameter is also a begin ... end block. Each entry declares a default value and optional type for a sampler option. Attributes are accessed with MOI.get, MOI.set, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute, JuMP's set_optimizer_attribute, or the generated typed attribute.\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name    = \"Super Sampler\"\n    version = v\"1.0.2\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer = 1_000\n        SuperAttribute::String = \"super\"\n    end\nend\n\nIn the example above, users can write either:\n\nMOI.set(sampler, NumberOfReads(), 2_000)\nMOI.set(sampler, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"num_reads\"), 2_000)\n\nEvery generated optimizer also supports QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads() and the raw key \"final_num_reads\". \"num_reads\" controls reads used by a sampler's internal search or optimizer evaluations. \"final_num_reads\" controls the reads used to build the returned SampleSet. If \"final_num_reads\" is unset, call QUBODrivers.final_number_of_reads(sampler) to get the effective value; it defaults to \"num_reads\" when that attribute is supported.\n\nDeclare raw \"seed\" to opt into the standard QUBODrivers.RandomSeed() attribute:\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name = \"Seeded Sampler\"\n    attributes = begin\n        \"seed\"::Union{Integer,Nothing} = nothing\n    end\nend\n\nWhen a seed is configured, QUBODrivers records it under metadata[\"seeds\"][\"sampler\"] after sampling.\n\nEvery generated optimizer also supports QUBODrivers.AutoConfigure() and the raw key \"auto_configure\" (defaulting to false). Samplers that opt into problem-size parameter scaling implement QUBODrivers.supports_auto_configure(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = true and check QUBODrivers.has_explicit_raw_attr inside QUBODrivers.sample(sampler) so any explicitly set raw attribute (\"num_reads\", \"num_sweeps\", etc.) always takes precedence over auto-scaled values, recording the resolved problem-size scaling provenance under metadata[\"auto_configure\"] while preserving user-configured raw attributes across solves.\n\nGenerated optimizers also support a generic post-sampling hook through QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback() and the raw key \"post_sample_callback\". The callback is called after QUBODrivers.sample returns and before the final SampleSet is attached:\n\ncallback = function (sampleset, sampler)\n    QUBOTools.metadata(sampleset)[\"postprocess_note\"] = \"annotated\"\n\n    return nothing\nend\n\nMOI.set(sampler, QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback(), callback)\n\nThe callback receives a copy of the raw backend SampleSet and the sampler context. Metadata-only annotations are allowed by default. If the callback changes sample states, objective values, reads, sense, or domain, also set QUBODrivers.PostSampleTransform() or raw \"post_sample_transform\" to true and return the transformed SampleSet. Replacement SampleSets should carry over or intentionally rebuild the original metadata; otherwise backend origin, status, reads, timing, and diagnostics metadata from the raw output are lost. When transformed samples are emitted, QUBODrivers records postprocess metadata and preserves the raw sample frame and records in the emitted solution metadata.\n\nUse this hook for driver-level sample annotation, objective bookkeeping, or controlled repair of emitted samples. For objective bookkeeping, prefer the QUBOTools convention metadata(sampleset)[\"objectives\"][label]. If your QUBOTools version includes objective-bookkeeping helpers, prefer QUBOTools.annotate_objectives! and QUBOTools.verify_objective_values for this. Problem-specific repair algorithms and the reformulation metadata needed to implement them should live outside QUBODrivers. ToQUBO versions that expose reformulation metadata record it under metadata(QUBOTools.backend(model))[\"toqubo\"][\"reformulation\"] and expose helpers such as ToQUBO.project_original_state; QUBODrivers only provides the sampler-interface hook.","title":"Attributes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#The-[QUBODrivers.sample](@ref)-method","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"","title":"The QUBODrivers.sample method"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#The-SampleSet-collection","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"The QUBODrivers.sample method must return a QUBOTools.SampleSet{T}. A SampleSet collects QUBOTools.Sample entries together with metadata about the sampling run.\n\nBuild a SampleSet from a vector of samples and an optional metadata dictionary:\n\nsamples = QUBOTools.Sample{T,Int}[\n    QUBOTools.Sample{T,Int}(ψ, λ)   # state vector ψ, objective value λ\n    for (ψ, λ) in zip(states, values)\n]\n\nmetadata = Dict{String,Any}(\n    \"time\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"total\" => elapsed),\n)\n\nreturn QUBOTools.SampleSet(samples, metadata; sense = :min, domain = :bool)\n\nThe sense keyword (:min or :max) and domain (:bool or :spin) tell QUBOTools how to interpret the samples.\n\nThe metadata dictionary is the place to record backend status, timing, and diagnostics. QUBODrivers will add a total time and empty status string if they are missing, but backend-specific wrappers should provide as much useful metadata as their solver exposes. See the Metadata Schema for the normative benchmarking contract.\n\nQUBODrivers requires these top-level metadata keys for benchmark-ready driver results:\n\n\"origin\": human-readable driver or backend origin;\n\"algorithm\": dictionary with at least \"name\";\n\"backend\": dictionary with backend \"name\" and \"version\" when known;\n\"execution\": dictionary with \"mode\";\n\"optimizer\": dictionary with \"iterations\" and objective/backend \"evaluations\" actually performed, when known;\n\"reads\": dictionary with \"number_of_reads\" and \"final_number_of_reads\" counts actually consumed by internal search and final sample-set construction, respectively; if there is no separate internal phase, both values should be the same;\n\"seeds\": dictionary of sampler, model, optimizer, or backend seeds;\n\"status\" and \"termination_status\": raw and structured termination status.\n\nWhen a post-sampling callback runs, QUBODrivers records callback metadata under \"postprocess\" => \"callback\". If the callback transforms emitted samples, the raw sample frame and records are stored under \"postprocess\" => \"raw_samples\".","title":"The SampleSet collection"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#Sampler-checklist","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"Before releasing a driver wrapper, confirm that:\n\nQUBODrivers.validate_metadata(sampleset) returns an empty vector for normal results; see the Metadata Schema;\nstochastic samplers expose raw \"seed\" and support QUBODrivers.RandomSeed() when reproducibility is part of their contract;\nQUBODrivers.honors_final_reads and QUBODrivers.enforces_time_limit return true only for behavior the driver actually guarantees;\nQUBODrivers.supported_domains declares any non-binary discrete variable domains (:int for bounded integers, :set for explicit finite value sets via QUBODrivers.VariableEncodingSet) that the backend supports;\nMOI.get(sampler, MOI.SolveTimeSec()) reports effective backend time, while wall-clock time is available through QUBODrivers.total_time.","title":"Sampler checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#Non-Binary-Finite-Variable-Domains","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"By default, samplers declare QUBODrivers.supported_domains(::Type{<:AbstractSampler}) = Set([:bool, :spin]). Backends that natively accept bounded integer variables (MOI.Integer with finite bounds) or arbitrary finite integer value sets (QUBODrivers.VariableEncodingSet) can opt in:\n\nQUBODrivers.supported_domains(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = Set([:bool, :spin, :int, :set])\n\nInside QUBODrivers.sample(sampler):\n\nQUBODrivers.variable_domains returns the ordered finite integer value set U_i subseteq mathbbZ for each free variable in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order;\nQUBODrivers.variable_bounds and QUBODrivers.variable_num_levels return per-variable (lower, upper) bounds and level counts d_i = U_i;\nQUBODrivers.levels_to_values and QUBODrivers.values_to_levels translate between zero-based solver levels 0(d_i - 1) and variable values in U_i;\nQUBODrivers.polynomial_objective with shift = true substitutes x_i leftarrow x_i + l_i on contiguous integer domains so zero-based integer backends can evaluate coefficients directly on 0(u_i - l_i).","title":"Non-Binary Finite Variable Domains"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/4-setup/#A-complete-example","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"module SuperSampler\n\nimport QUBODrivers\nimport QUBODrivers: QUBOTools\nimport MathOptInterface as MOI\n\n@doc raw\"\"\"\n    SuperSampler.Optimizer\n\nThis sampler is super!\n\"\"\"\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name    = \"Super Sampler\"\n    version = v\"1.0.2\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer = 1_000\n        SuperAttribute::String = \"super\"\n    end\nend\n\nfunction QUBODrivers.sample(sampler::Optimizer{T}) where {T}\n    # ~ Is your annealer running on the Ising Model? Have this:\n    n, h, J, α, β = QUBOTools.ising(\n        sampler,\n        :dense; # Here we opt for a dense matrix representation\n        sense = :max,\n    )\n\n    # ~ Retrieve Attributes using MathOptInterface ~ #\n    num_reads  = MOI.get(sampler, NumberOfReads())\n    final_reads = MOI.get(sampler, QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads())\n    super_attr = MOI.get(sampler, SuperAttribute())\n\n    # ~ Do some sampling ~ #\n    samples = QUBOTools.Sample{T,Int}[]\n\n    clock = @timed for _ = 1:final_reads\n        ψ = super_sample(n, h, J, super_attr)\n        λ = QUBOTools.value(ψ, h, J, α, β)\n\n        s = QUBOTools.Sample{T,Int}(ψ, λ)\n\n        push!(samples, s)\n    end\n\n    # ~ Store some metadata ~ #\n    metadata = Dict{String,Any}(\n        \"origin\"    => \"Super Sampler @ SuperBackend\",\n        \"algorithm\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"name\" => \"Super Sampler\"),\n        \"backend\"   => Dict{String,Any}(\"name\" => \"SuperBackend\", \"version\" => nothing),\n        \"execution\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"mode\" => \"sampling\"),\n        \"optimizer\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"iterations\" => nothing, \"evaluations\" => final_reads),\n        \"reads\"     => Dict{String,Any}(\n            \"number_of_reads\"       => final_reads,\n            \"final_number_of_reads\" => final_reads,\n        ),\n        \"seeds\"     => Dict{String,Any}(),\n        \"status\"    => \"locally_solved\",\n        \"super_attr\" => super_attr,\n        \"time\"      => Dict{String,Any}(\"effective\" => clock.time),\n    )\n\n    # ~ Return a SampleSet ~ #\n    return QUBOTools.SampleSet(samples, metadata; sense=:max, domain=:spin)\nend\n\nfunction super_sample(n, h, J, super_attr)\n    # ~ Do some super sampling (using C/C++) ~ #\n    ψ = ccall(\n        :super_sample,\n        Vector{Int},\n        (\n            Cint,\n            Ptr{Float64},\n            Ptr{Ptr{Float64}},\n            Cstring\n        ),\n        n,\n        h,\n        J,\n        super_attr,\n    )\n\n    return ψ\nend\n\nend # module","title":"A complete example"},{"category":"macro","location":"manual/4-setup/#QUBODrivers.@setup","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"QUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name = \"Solver Name\"\n    version = v\"1.0.0\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer = 1_000\n    end\nend\n\nDeclare a QUBODrivers sampler optimizer type.\n\nThe macro creates a mutable Optimizer{T} <: QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler{T}, QUBOTools model storage, MOI optimizer metadata, raw attribute storage, and MOI.get/MOI.set/MOI.supports methods for declared attributes.\n\nThe setup block accepts:\n\nname: required solver name returned by MOI.SolverName;\nversion: optional VersionNumber, defaulting to the QUBODrivers package version;\nattributes: optional block of solver attributes.\n\nAttributes are assignments to default values. They may be typed with ::T, exposed as typed MOI attributes, exposed as raw string attributes, or exposed as both:\n\n\"num_reads\" = 1_000\n\"num_reads\"::Integer = 1_000\nNumberOfReads = 1_000\nNumberOfReads::Integer = 1_000\nNumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"] = 1_000\nNumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer = 1_000\n\nExample\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name       = \"Super Sampler\"\n    version    = v\"1.0.2\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer  = 1_000\n        SuperAttribute[\"super_attr\"]         = nothing\n        MegaAttribute::Union{String,Nothing} = \"mega\"\n    end\nend\n\nAfter setup, implement QUBODrivers.sample for the generated optimizer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.@setup"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/4-setup/#QUBODrivers.sample","page":"Sampler Setup","text":"sample(::AbstractSampler{T})::SampleSet{T} where {T}\n\nRun the backend sampler and return a QUBOTools.SampleSet.\n\nSampler packages implement this method for their optimizer type. The method should read the model from the sampler, read any MOI or raw optimizer attributes it needs, call the backend, and return a SampleSet{T} whose samples use the same sense and domain as the backend output.\n\nMOI.optimize! calls this method and attaches the returned sample set to the optimizer. If the returned metadata does not include a \"time\" dictionary with a \"total\" entry, or does not include \"status\", QUBODrivers fills those fields with default values. It then validates the benchmarking metadata schema and emits a warning, not an error, for missing or malformed fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.sample"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/2-interface/#Sampler-Interface","page":"Sampler Interface","text":"","title":"Sampler Interface"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/2-interface/#QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler","page":"Sampler Interface","text":"AbstractSampler{T} <: MOI.AbstractOptimizer\n\nAbstract supertype for QUBODrivers sampler optimizers.\n\nConcrete sampler optimizers are usually generated with QUBODrivers.@setup. They are MathOptInterface optimizers that accept QUBO or Ising models with binary or spin variables and return one or more sampled states through standard MOI result attributes.\n\nThe type parameter T is the numeric coefficient type used by the internal QUBOTools.Model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/2-interface/#QUBODrivers.set_model!","page":"Sampler Interface","text":"set_model!(sampler::AbstractSampler{T}, model::QUBOTools.Model{VI,T,Int}) where {T}\n\nStore the QUBOTools model backing a sampler.\n\nThe @setup macro provides this method for generated optimizer types. Custom sampler types that do not use @setup must provide it so that MOI.copy_to can transfer JuMP/MOI models into the sampler before optimization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.set_model!"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-intro/#Introduction","page":"Introduction","text":"","title":"Introduction"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-intro/#QUBO","page":"Introduction","text":"An optimization problem is in its QUBO form if it is written as\n\nbeginarrayrl\n           min  alpha left mathbfxmathbfQmathbfx + mathbfellmathbfx + beta right \n    textst  mathbfx in S cong mathbbB^n\nendarray\n\nwith linear terms mathbfell in mathbbR^n and quadratic mathbfQ in mathbbR^n times n. alpha beta in mathbbR are, respectively, the scaling and offset factors.\n\nThe MOI-JuMP optimizers defined using the QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler{T} <: MOI.AbstractOptimizer interface only support models given in the QUBO form. QUBODrivers.jl employs QUBOTools on many tasks involving data management and querying. It is worth taking a look at the QUBOTools docs.","title":"QUBO"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-intro/#Package-Layout","page":"Introduction","text":"The package has three main layers:\n\nuser-facing JuMP/MOI optimizers, including the built-in utility samplers;\nsampler-author tools, centered on QUBODrivers.@setup and QUBODrivers.sample;\ntesting and benchmarking helpers that exercise the sampler interface.\n\nAll solver-specific wrappers should preserve the same public contract: accept a QUBO or Ising model through MOI, call their backend in QUBODrivers.sample, and return a QUBOTools sample set that JuMP users can query through standard result APIs.","title":"Package Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/1-intro/#Table-of-Contents","page":"Introduction","text":"Pages = [\"2-solve.md\", \"3-samplers.md\", \"4-setup.md\", \"7-integration.md\", \"5-tests.md\", \"6-benchmarks.md\", \"8-api.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Table of Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Implementing-a-Sampler","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"This walkthrough builds a minimal sampler that works through the same public interfaces used by package wrappers. It uses exhaustive search so the example is self-contained; a production wrapper can replace that inner loop with a call to an external library, service, or device.","title":"Implementing a Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Minimal-module","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"A QUBODrivers sampler needs an optimizer type and a QUBODrivers.sample method. The QUBODrivers.@setup macro creates the optimizer type, standard MOI methods, and attribute storage.\n\nmodule DemoSampler\n\nimport QUBODrivers\nimport QUBODrivers: MOI, QUBOTools, Sample, SampleSet\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name    = \"Demo Sampler\"\n    version = v\"0.1.0\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer = 1\n    end\nend\n\nfunction QUBODrivers.sample(sampler::Optimizer{T}) where {T}\n    n, linear, quadratic, scale, offset = QUBOTools.qubo(\n        sampler,\n        :dict;\n        sense = :min,\n    )\n\n    num_reads = MOI.get(sampler, NumberOfReads())\n    final_reads = MOI.get(sampler, QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads())\n    if num_reads < 1\n        error(\"'num_reads' must be a positive integer\")\n    end\n    if final_reads < 1\n        error(\"'final_num_reads' must be a positive integer\")\n    end\n\n    best_state = Vector{Int}()\n    best_value = nothing\n\n    for state in Iterators.product(ntuple(_ -> (0, 1), n)...)\n        candidate_state = collect(state)\n        candidate_value = QUBOTools.value(\n            candidate_state,\n            linear,\n            quadratic,\n            scale,\n            offset,\n        )\n\n        if isnothing(best_value) || candidate_value < best_value\n            best_state = candidate_state\n            best_value = candidate_value\n        end\n    end\n\n    samples = [\n        Sample{T,Int}(copy(best_state), best_value)\n        for _ in 1:final_reads\n    ]\n\n    metadata = Dict{String,Any}(\n        \"origin\"    => \"Demo Sampler\",\n        \"algorithm\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"name\" => \"Demo Sampler\"),\n        \"backend\"   => Dict{String,Any}(\"name\" => \"Demo Sampler\", \"version\" => v\"0.1.0\"),\n        \"execution\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"mode\" => \"exhaustive_search\"),\n        \"optimizer\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"iterations\" => nothing, \"evaluations\" => final_reads),\n        \"reads\"     => Dict{String,Any}(\n            \"number_of_reads\"       => final_reads,\n            \"final_number_of_reads\" => final_reads,\n        ),\n        \"seeds\"     => Dict{String,Any}(),\n        \"time\"      => Dict{String,Any}(\"effective\" => 0.0),\n        \"status\"    => \"optimal\",\n    )\n\n    return SampleSet{T}(samples, metadata; sense = :min, domain = :bool)\nend\n\nend\n\nnothing","title":"Minimal module"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Use-it-from-JuMP","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"The generated Optimizer type is an MOI.AbstractOptimizer, so it can be passed directly to JuMP.Model.\n\nusing JuMP\n\nmodel = Model(DemoSampler.Optimizer)\nset_optimizer_attribute(model, \"num_reads\", 2)\n\n@variable(model, x[1:2], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, -x[1] - 2x[2] + 3x[1] * x[2])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\n(objective_value(model), round.(Int, value.(x)), result_count(model))\n(-2.0, [0, 1], 1)","title":"Use it from JuMP"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Implementation-checklist","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"When adapting the example for a real sampler:\n\nchoose the QUBOTools representation that matches the backend, such as QUBOTools.qubo(sampler, :dict; sense = :min) or QUBOTools.ising(sampler, :dense; sense = :max);\nread user-facing options through MOI attributes generated by @setup;\nuse QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads() for the returned sample count when a backend has separate search and final sampling phases;\nconvert backend outputs into Sample{T,Int} entries;\nreturn a SampleSet{T} with the correct sense and domain;\ninclude useful metadata such as timing, backend status, and backend version;\nleave generic post-sampling annotation or repair to QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback() when users need a configurable hook;\nuse QUBOTools objective metadata and ToQUBO reformulation metadata for objective bookkeeping, original-variable projection, and encoding-specific auxiliary handling;\nrun QUBODrivers.test(YourSampler.Optimizer) in the package test suite.\n\nThe built-in RandomSampler and ExactSampler implementations are compact references for these same steps.","title":"Implementation checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Polynomial-Objectives-(HUBO-/-PUBO)","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"Samplers that natively accept higher-order polynomial objectives (such as HUBO or PUBO solvers) can opt into QUBODrivers' polynomial objective bridge by specializing QUBODrivers.supports_polynomial_objective:\n\nQUBODrivers.supports_polynomial_objective(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = true\n\nWhen enabled, the sampler accepts MOI.ScalarNonlinearFunction and QUBODrivers.ScalarPolynomialFunction objectives in addition to linear and quadratic MOI objectives. Inside QUBODrivers.sample, call QUBODrivers.polynomial_objective to retrieve a MultivariatePolynomials.AbstractPolynomial{T} over the free variables in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order (or QUBODrivers.polynomial_function to retrieve the MOI QUBODrivers.ScalarPolynomialFunction wrapper), with fixed variables substituted and constant offsets preserved. Non-polynomial operators (such as sin, division / by zero or a non-constant expression, or non-integer/negative ^) throw QUBODrivers.InvalidNLExpression. Samplers that do not opt in keep supports_polynomial_objective == false and remain on the quadratic QUBO/Ising lowering path.","title":"Polynomial Objectives (HUBO / PUBO)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Package-a-sampler-interface","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"Once the sampler module works, package it like a normal Julia interface package. The smallest useful repository usually has src/, test/, Project.toml, and GitHub automation for tests and releases. Prefer this checklist over a generated template: QUBODrivers interfaces vary from single-file wrappers to multi-module hardware clients and Python-backed packages.\n\nUse src/library/drivers/ExactSampler.jl as the canonical copy-from skeleton for the optimizer declaration, the QUBODrivers.sample method, and metadata shape. Then compare against real interfaces such as DWave.jl and JuliQAOAOpt.jl for package layouts that include external backends.","title":"Package a sampler interface"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Project.toml","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"A standalone interface package should depend on QUBODrivers, QUBOTools, and MathOptInterface. Add backend-specific dependencies only when the sampler calls them directly.\n\nname = \"MySampler\"\nuuid = \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\"\nauthors = [\"Your Name <you@example.com>\"]\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\n\n[deps]\nMathOptInterface = \"b8f27783-ece8-5eb3-8dc8-9495eed66fee\"\nQUBODrivers = \"a3f166f7-2cd3-47b6-9e1e-6fbfe0449eb0\"\nQUBOTools = \"60eb5b62-0a39-4ddc-84c5-97d2adff9319\"\n\n[compat]\nMathOptInterface = \"1\"\nQUBODrivers = \"0.6\"\nQUBOTools = \"0.10, 0.11, 0.12, 0.13, 0.14\"\njulia = \"1.10\"\n\nKeep [compat] bounds explicit. They define what users and CI can install, and they are also what registry review checks before accepting a release.\n\nReplace the placeholder uuid with a real value generated in Julia, for example using UUIDs; uuid4().","title":"Project.toml"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Tests","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"Put the public interface checks in test/runtests.jl. The QUBODrivers.test extension loads when both QUBODrivers and Julia's Test standard library are available.\n\nusing Test\nusing MySampler\nusing QUBODrivers\n\n@testset \"MySampler\" begin\n    QUBODrivers.test(MySampler.Optimizer)\nend\n\nFor samplers that need deterministic options or credentials-free operation, pass a setup function or disable example problems as shown in Test Suite. Keep backend integration tests separate when they require secrets, paid hardware, or long runtimes.","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Public-composition-pattern","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"A composite sampler can implement QUBODrivers.sample and the public QUBODrivers.set_model! hook, using @setup for its optimizer or for model storage. Read the backing model through QUBOTools.backend and public model queries. Keep decomposition policy in the standalone package.\n\nAccept a zero-argument factory that constructs a fresh, empty child optimizer for each call. Configure child-specific attributes inside that factory through MOI.set, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute, or MOI.OptimizerWithAttributes; use MOI.instantiate when a wrapper needs bridges. Check support for the domain, objective and attributes required by the child contract.\n\nusing QUBODrivers\nimport MathOptInterface as MOI\n\nchild_factory = () -> QUBODrivers.ExactSampler.Optimizer()\nchild = child_factory()\n(child isa MOI.AbstractOptimizer, MOI.is_empty(child))\n(true, true)\n\nCopy each child problem with MOI.copy_to and retain its returned index map for MOI.VariablePrimal queries. Explicitly include isolated variables. Reconstruct complete global assignments, including fixed variables, and independently evaluate the original model objective with its sense, domain, scale and offset. Child-reported objectives alone cannot validate reconstruction. For ToQUBO, this original model is the compiled QUBO; source feasibility and source objective evaluation remain a separate compiler/user responsibility.\n\nUse public MOI.ResultCount, MOI.PrimalStatus, MOI.TerminationStatus, and result queries to validate a child before committing its candidates. A raw metadata status string is diagnostic, not a certificate. A completed exhaustive ExactSampler solve exposes public OPTIMAL; failed solves and transformed post-sample results do not retain that certificate. QUBODrivers 0.6.5 and earlier returned LOCALLY_SOLVED despite exhaustive completion; this was a public-status defect, not an intended algorithm limitation. Consumers using that release must retain its observed status until adopting a version containing the fix. Independent component certificates require valid public OPTIMAL results for every component; exact neighborhood solves in coupled sweeps do not prove a global optimum. Preserve limits, interruptions and failures conservatively, exposing only validated results and clearing stale results/proofs on a new invocation.\n\nQUBODecomposition's public implementation demonstrates this pattern using released QUBODrivers 0.6.5 and QUBOTools 0.16.2 interfaces. No new upstream hook, schema or routing trait is needed for this consumer. Its whole-model example and serial example show factory/budget configuration. See Composite accounting for metadata and Composite conformance evidence for the audited tests.","title":"Public composition pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Continuous-integration-and-maintenance","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"Use CI to exercise the package on the Julia versions in [compat]. A typical GitHub Actions workflow checks out the repository, installs Julia with julia-actions/setup-julia, enables julia-actions/cache, runs julia-actions/julia-buildpkg, and runs julia-actions/julia-runtest.\n\nAdd Dependabot for the package environments that exist in the repository. Common entries are:\n\nthe root Julia package environment at /;\n/docs if the package builds Documenter docs;\n/test if tests use a separate test environment;\nGitHub Actions updates at /.\n\nIf the package is registered and should publish Git tags automatically, add TagBot with JuliaRegistries/TagBot. Registration is optional for sampler interfaces, and TagBot is only useful once releases are driven by registry events.","title":"Continuous integration and maintenance"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Installation-and-registration","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"Unregistered sampler packages can be installed directly from their repository URL. This is the default fallback path while an interface is private, experimental, or too specialized for registration.\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/Owner/MySampler.jl\")\n\nWhen the package is stable enough for wider use, register it with Julia's General registry using Registrator. Before registering, confirm the package has a unique UUID, an incremented version, explicit [compat] bounds, passing CI, and documentation that points users back to this QUBODrivers interface guide.","title":"Installation and registration"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/7-integration/#Registering-the-package-with-QUBODrivers-docs","page":"Implementing a Sampler","text":"If the sampler package is public and current in the JuliaQUBO or SECQUOIA organizations, open a QUBODrivers documentation PR that adds it to the external sampler table in docs/src/manual/3-samplers.md. That table is maintained as the reviewed source of truth rather than generated from GitHub search.\n\nInclude:\n\nthe package repository URL;\nthe public solver type, such as Package.Optimizer or Package.Submodule.Optimizer;\nthe source file path that defines the optimizer;\na note when the package is deprecated, renamed, experimental, private, or a compatibility shim.\n\nFor documentation-only registration PRs, run:\n\njulia --project=docs/ docs/make.jl --skip-deploy","title":"Registering the package with QUBODrivers docs"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Optional-CUDA-Q-QAOA-Sampler-Design","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"This page records the contract and package-boundary design for an optional CUDA-Q Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) sampler wrapper for the JuliaQUBO ecosystem (JuliaQUBO/QUBODrivers.jl#88). It defines package ownership, the minimum QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler surface, target and precision semantics, credential-free CPU conformance testing, and the Python/CUDA-Q environment probe required before runtime publication in a dedicated wrapper repository.\n\nnote: Design Status and Package Boundary\nQUBODrivers.jl hosts this interface specification and conformance checklist only; it does not depend on PythonCall at runtime for CUDA-Q, import cudaq, or bundle GPU/vendor SDKs in core QUBODrivers.jl or QUBO.jl. Once the standalone repository is bootstrapped and passes conformance, it will be added to the External Sampler Packages table in docs/src/manual/3-samplers.md.","title":"Optional CUDA-Q QAOA Sampler Design"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Ownership-and-Package-Placement","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"Proposed package and module: A standalone optional wrapper package, CUDAQOpt.jl (hosted under JuliaQUBO or SECQUOIA), exposing CUDAQOpt.QAOA.Optimizer{T} <: QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler{T} generated via QUBODrivers.@setup.\nZero vendor leakage into core packages: QUBODrivers.jl, QUBOTools.jl, ToQUBO.jl, and QUBODecomposition.jl remain free of CUDA, CUDA.jl, or Python cudaq dependencies.\nDecomposition compatibility: CUDAQOpt.QAOA.Optimizer must compose as a child solver of QUBODecomposition.Optimizer through the standard zero-argument child factory () -> ... and public MOI.copy_to / QUBODrivers.sample interface, without CUDA-specific branches in the decomposition controller (see Public composition pattern).\nRelationship to instructional notebooks: Existing Python CUDA-Q QAOA material in QUBONotebooks (tests/test_cudaq_qaoa.py, tracked under JuliaQUBO/QUBONotebooks#137) remains instructional Python code and is not the Julia MOI driver.\nUpstream reference attribution: Where Hamiltonian assembly, parameter optimization, or bitstring decoding adapts patterns from alpha-unito/QSplit and qsplit/adapters/nvidia/util.py, the wrapper repository must preserve the QSplit MIT license notice and record the adapted revision (4da64b072e702953038addd51cdf54f97f0f9516).","title":"Ownership and Package Placement"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Minimum-Sampler-Surface-and-Attributes","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"The optimizer must be declared with QUBODrivers.@setup and expose explicit MOI / raw optimizer attributes for circuit depth, target selection, precision, shot budgets, classical optimization budget, and seeding:\n\nAttribute / Raw Key Type & Default Contract\n\"p\" Integer = 1 QAOA circuit depth (p ge 1), parameterizing 2p variational angles (gamma_1 dots gamma_p beta_1 dots beta_p).\n\"target\" String = \"qpp-cpu\" Explicit CUDA-Q simulation or hardware target (for example \"qpp-cpu\", \"nvidia\", \"tensornet\").\n\"target_option\" Union{String,Nothing} = nothing Explicit target option or floating-point precision selector (such as \"fp32\" or \"fp64\" when supported by the target).\n\"allow_target_fallback\" Bool = false Whether the driver may fall back according to an explicit target list when the requested GPU target or precision is unavailable at target-initialization time.\n\"num_reads\" Integer = 1_024 Shot count (shots_count) per circuit evaluation during variational parameter optimization.\nQUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads() / \"final_num_reads\" Union{Integer,Nothing} = nothing Shot count used to sample the final optimized QAOA circuit (honors_final_reads(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = true; defaults to \"num_reads\" when unset).\n\"optimizer\" String = \"COBYLA\" Classical outer-loop optimizer name (cudaq.optimizers or scipy.optimize.minimize).\n\"max_iterations\" Integer = 100 Maximum classical optimizer iterations (max_iterations >= 1).\n\"initial_point\" Union{Vector{Float64},Nothing} = nothing Optional length-2p initial parameter vector; when nothing, initialized deterministically from \"seed\".\nQUBODrivers.RandomSeed() / \"seed\" Union{Integer,Nothing} = nothing Seed forwarded to cudaq.set_random_seed(seed) and initial-point / classical-optimizer RNG (supports_seed(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = true).","title":"Minimum Sampler Surface and Attributes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Model-Lowering,-Bit-Ordering,-and-Objective-Evaluation","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"Variable ordering and isolated variables: Retrieve the internal model in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order (1:n) using n, h, J, α, β = QUBOTools.ising(sampler, :dict; sense = :min) (which uses QUBOTools's spin convention s_i = 2x_i - 1 in -1 +1, i.e., x_i = (1 + s_i)2 with x_i = 0 leftrightarrow s_i = -1 and x_i = 1 leftrightarrow s_i = +1). All n free logical variables—including isolated variables with zero linear and quadratic coefficients—must be allocated a qubit index q_i-1 (0:(n - 1)), initialized in +rangle = H0rangle, acted on by the mixer unitary U_B(beta_l) = prod_i=0^n-1 R_X(2beta_l q_i), and measured.\nCost Hamiltonian: Under standard computational-basis bit encoding x_irangle (b_i = text0 leftrightarrow x_i = 0, b_i = text1 leftrightarrow x_i = 1), the Pauli-Z operator satisfies Z_i-1x_irangle = (1 - 2x_i)x_irangle = -s_ix_irangle. Substituting s_i = -Z_i-1 into the QUBOTools.ising form (equivalently substituting x_i = (I - Z_i-1)2 into QUBOTools.qubo) yields the Pauli-Z spin Hamiltonian:\nH_C = -sum_i=1^n h_i Z_i-1 + sum_1 le i  j le n J_ij Z_i-1 Z_j-1\nbuilt using cudaq.SpinOperator terms (-h_i * spin.z(i - 1) and J_{ij} * spin.z(i - 1) * spin.z(j - 1)). For n = 0 (all variables fixed or empty model), bypass CUDA-Q circuit execution and emit a single Sample{T,Int} with empty state Int[], objective value alpha beta, and final_num_reads reads.\nBitstring decoding and independent energy scoring: CUDA-Q cudaq.sample(kernel, ...; shots_count = final_num_reads) returns a SampleResult mapping measured bitstrings b in 0 1^n in register declaration order (q[0] through q[n - 1]) to shot counts c_b ge 1 with sum_b c_b = textfinal_num_reads.\nMap each measured bit b_k in text0 text1 to:\nx_k = (b_k == '1' ? 1 : 0) when constructing a :bool SampleSet (domain = :bool), scored via lambda = textQUBOToolsvalue(x L Q alpha_q beta_q) from QUBOTools.qubo(sampler, :dict; sense = :min); or\ns_k = (b_k == '1' ? 1 : -1) = -z_k when constructing a :spin SampleSet (domain = :spin), scored via lambda = textQUBOToolsvalue(s h J alpha beta) from QUBOTools.ising(sampler, :dict; sense = :min), so that both QUBOTools.value and QUBOTools.attach!'s SpinDomain => BoolDomain cast (x_k = (s_k + 1)2) agree identically.\nConstruct QUBOTools.Sample{T,Int}(ψ, λ, count) so shot multiplicities are preserved as sample read counts.","title":"Model Lowering, Bit Ordering, and Objective Evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Target-Isolation,-Precision-Policy,-and-Error-Semantics","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"Global target-state isolation: In Python cudaq, cudaq.set_target(...) mutates process-global state. The Julia wrapper must guard target selection, seed assignment, parameter optimization, and final sampling with a module-level ReentrantLock (and explicitly set the configured target at the start of every QUBODrivers.sample(sampler) call) so sequential or multi-task solves in the same Julia process cannot leak target or precision state across optimizer instances.\nNo silent precision or hardware downgrade: Unlike QSplit's automatic nvidia (fp64) -> nvidia (fp32) -> qpp-cpu loop, CUDAQOpt.QAOA.Optimizer defaults to \"allow_target_fallback\" = false:\nIf the caller requests \"target\" = \"nvidia\" and \"target_option\" = \"fp64\", any missing-target, unsupported-precision, or GPU out-of-memory failure must not silently switch to \"fp32\" or \"qpp-cpu\".\nOnly when \"allow_target_fallback\" = true is explicitly set may target initialization fall back across a documented candidate sequence, recording both \"requested_target\", \"selected_target\", \"fallback_applied\" = true, and \"fallback_reason\" in metadata[\"cudaq\"].\nMid-solve execution failures (such as GPU out-of-memory during circuit simulation or invalid kernel parameters) must never trigger a silent retry on a different target.\nActionable GPU memory and capacity failures: Before and during execution, if cudaq raises an out-of-memory, device-capacity, or invalid-target error, the wrapper must clear any stale solution on the optimizer, set MOI.TerminationStatus() to MOI.MEMORY_LIMIT (for memory / qubit-capacity exhaustion) or MOI.OTHER_ERROR, and rethrow an exception whose message includes the requested \"target\", \"target_option\", logical variable count n, and underlying CUDA-Q error text.","title":"Target Isolation, Precision Policy, and Error Semantics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Metadata-Contract","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"Every normal solve must pass QUBODrivers.validate_metadata with zero violations and populate:\n\n\"origin\": \"CUDAQOpt.QAOA @ cudaq\"\n\"algorithm\": Dict(\"name\" => \"QAOA\", \"p\" => p, \"optimizer\" => optimizer_name)\n\"backend\": Dict(\"name\" => \"cudaq\", \"version\" => cudaq_version)\n\"execution\": Dict(\"mode\" => selected_target == \"qpp-cpu\" ? \"local_cpu_simulator\" : \"cudaq_target\")\n\"optimizer\": Dict(\"iterations\" => iterations_performed, \"evaluations\" => circuit_evaluations)\n\"reads\": Dict(\"number_of_reads\" => num_reads, \"final_number_of_reads\" => final_num_reads)\n\"seeds\": Dict(\"sampler\" => seed) when seeded\n\"cudaq\":\nDict{String,Any}(\n    \"requested_target\" => requested_target,\n    \"selected_target\"  => selected_target,\n    \"target_option\"    => target_option,\n    \"precision\"        => resolved_precision,\n    \"fallback_applied\" => fallback_applied,\n    \"fallback_reason\"  => fallback_reason,\n    \"num_qubits\"       => n,\n)","title":"Metadata Contract"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Credential-Free-CPU-Test-Route-and-Environment-Probe","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"","title":"Credential-Free CPU Test Route and Environment Probe"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Supported-Python-/-CUDA-Q-Environment-Probe","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"Before running wrapper conformance tests or GPU smoke checks, verify the local Python and cudaq installation with the following standalone probe (requiring no quantum hardware credentials):\n\nusing PythonCall\n\nfunction probe_cudaq_environment()\n    sys = pyimport(\"sys\")\n    py_version = pyconvert(String, sys.version)\n    cudaq = try\n        pyimport(\"cudaq\")\n    catch err\n        error(\n            \"Python package `cudaq` is not installed in the active PythonCall environment \" *\n            \"(Python $py_version). Install `cudaq >= 0.8` on a supported platform \" *\n            \"(Linux x86_64/aarch64 or macOS arm64 for CPU targets) before running CUDAQOpt.\",\n        )\n    end\n\n    cudaq_version = hasproperty(cudaq, :__version__) ?\n        pyconvert(String, cudaq.__version__) : \"unknown\"\n    has_qpp_cpu = pyconvert(Bool, cudaq.has_target(\"qpp-cpu\"))\n    has_nvidia  = pyconvert(Bool, cudaq.has_target(\"nvidia\"))\n    num_gpus    = hasproperty(cudaq, :num_available_gpus) ?\n        pyconvert(Int, cudaq.num_available_gpus()) : 0\n\n    return (\n        python_version = py_version,\n        cudaq_version  = cudaq_version,\n        has_qpp_cpu    = has_qpp_cpu,\n        has_nvidia     = has_nvidia,\n        num_gpus       = num_gpus,\n    )\nend","title":"Supported Python / CUDA-Q Environment Probe"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cudaq-design/#Conformance-Test-Plan-for-the-Standalone-Package","page":"CUDA-Q Sampler Design","text":"Credential-free CPU conformance (\"qpp-cpu\"):\nRun QUBODrivers.test(CUDAQOpt.QAOA.Optimizer; examples = true, benchmark_conformance = true) configured with \"target\" => \"qpp-cpu\", \"p\" => 1, \"num_reads\" => 64, \"max_iterations\" => 5, and \"seed\" => 42.\nVerify tiny 2- to 4-variable QUBO and Ising models with non-zero scale and offset, MOI.MIN_SENSE and MOI.MAX_SENSE, isolated variables, fixed variables, and non-monotonic MOI.VariableIndex insertion order against QUBODrivers.ExactSampler.Optimizer.\nTarget-isolation and fallback unit tests:\nVerify that two optimizer instances configured with different targets or options preserve their respective \"selected_target\" and \"precision\" in metadata[\"cudaq\"].\nVerify that requesting a nonexistent target or unavailable GPU target with \"allow_target_fallback\" = false throws an actionable error and sets MOI.TerminationStatus() to MOI.OTHER_ERROR (or MOI.MEMORY_LIMIT on simulated capacity errors) without silently falling back to \"qpp-cpu\".\nDecomposition child integration test:\nVerify that CUDAQOpt.QAOA.Optimizer works as the child factory of QUBODecomposition.Optimizer on a small multi-component and sweep model.\nOpt-in GPU smoke test:\nGate \"target\" => \"nvidia\" tests behind probe.has_nvidia && probe.num_gpus > 0 (and an explicit CI/local environment variable such as JULIA_CUDAQ_TEST_GPU=true) so standard CPU CI remains deterministic and credential-free.","title":"Conformance Test Plan for the Standalone Package"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#Attributes","page":"Attribute System","text":"","title":"Attributes"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#API","page":"Attribute System","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#Standard-sampler-attributes","page":"Attribute System","text":"Generated optimizers created with QUBODrivers.@setup support QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads() and the raw key \"final_num_reads\".\n\nUse \"num_reads\" for reads spent during a sampler's internal search, optimization, or objective evaluations. Use \"final_num_reads\" for reads used to build the returned SampleSet. If \"final_num_reads\" is unset, the effective final read count defaults to \"num_reads\" when the sampler supports that attribute.\n\nBuilt-in samplers that do not have a separate final sampling phase document the attribute as ignored and report their fixed returned read count in metadata.\n\nGenerated optimizers also support QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback() and the raw key \"post_sample_callback\". The callback receives a copied SampleSet and the sampler context after raw sampling. Metadata-only annotations are allowed by default. To emit changed sample states, values, reads, sense, or domain, set QUBODrivers.PostSampleTransform() or raw \"post_sample_transform\" to true and return the transformed SampleSet. Replacement SampleSets should carry over or intentionally rebuild raw backend metadata. Objective annotations should follow QUBOTools' \"objectives\" metadata convention, and reformulation-specific projection or repair data should come from ToQUBO/QUBOTools metadata rather than from QUBODrivers.","title":"Standard sampler attributes"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#An-advanced-example","page":"Attribute System","text":"module SuperSampler\n\nimport QUBODrivers\nimport QUBODrivers: QUBOTools\nimport MathOptInterface as MOI\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name    = \"Super Sampler\"\n    version = v\"1.0.2\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer  = 100_000\n        SuperAttribute[\"super_attr\"]::String = \"super\"\n    end\nend\n\nfunction MOI.set(sampler::Optimizer, attr::raw_attr\"\", value)\n    if !(value isa Integer)\n        error(\"'num_reads' must be an integer\")\n    else\n        QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!(sampler, attr, value)\n    end\n\n    return nothing\nend\n\nfunction MOI.set(sampler::Optimizer, attr::raw_attr\"super_attr\", value)\n    if !(value isa AbstractString)\n        error(\"'super_attr' must be a string\")\n    elseif !(value ∈ (\"super\", \"ultra\", \"mega\"))\n        error(\"'super_attr' must be one of the following: 'super', 'ultra', 'mega'\")\n    else\n        QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!(sampler, attr, value)\n    end\n\n    return nothing\nend\n\nend # SuperSampler module","title":"An advanced example"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.SamplerAttribute","page":"Attribute System","text":"SamplerAttribute\n\nAbstract supertype for optimizer attributes declared by QUBODrivers samplers.\n\nAttributes generated by QUBODrivers.@setup, such as NumberOfReads, subtype SamplerAttribute and can be used with MOI.get, MOI.set, and MOI.supports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.SamplerAttribute"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads","page":"Attribute System","text":"FinalNumberOfReads()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute for the number of reads used to build the final emitted SampleSet.\n\nNumberOfReads remains the convention for reads used by a sampler's internal search, optimization, or objective evaluations. FinalNumberOfReads separates the number of reads returned to users from those internal evaluations. If the final read count is unset (nothing), the effective final read count defaults to the sampler's raw \"num_reads\" attribute when that attribute is supported.\n\nGenerated optimizers created by QUBODrivers.@setup support this attribute and the raw \"final_num_reads\" key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.final_number_of_reads","page":"Attribute System","text":"final_number_of_reads(sampler)\n\nReturn the effective final read count for sampler.\n\nThis is equivalent to MOI.get(sampler, FinalNumberOfReads()) for samplers generated by QUBODrivers.@setup. If \"final_num_reads\" is unset, the returned value falls back to raw \"num_reads\" when supported; otherwise it is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.final_number_of_reads"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback","page":"Attribute System","text":"PostSampleCallback()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute for a callback applied after backend sampling and before the final SampleSet is attached to the optimizer.\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"post_sample_callback\". The callback is called as callback(sampleset, sampler), where sampleset is a copy of the raw backend output and sampler is the optimizer/model context. The callback may mutate the copied sample-set metadata and return nothing, or return a SampleSet to replace the emitted samples. Replacing or changing sample states, values, reads, sense, or domain requires PostSampleTransform to be set to true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.PostSampleTransform","page":"Attribute System","text":"PostSampleTransform()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute controlling whether PostSampleCallback may transform emitted samples.\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"post_sample_transform\", and the default value is false. When set to true, a post-sample callback may return a replacement SampleSet. QUBODrivers records callback metadata and preserves the raw samples in the emitted solution metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.PostSampleTransform"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.post_sample_callback","page":"Attribute System","text":"post_sample_callback(sampler)\n\nReturn the configured post-sample callback for sampler, or nothing when no callback is configured or the sampler does not support the generic callback attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.post_sample_callback"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.post_sample_transform","page":"Attribute System","text":"post_sample_transform(sampler)::Bool\n\nReturn whether the configured post-sample callback may transform emitted sample states, values, reads, sense, or domain. Samplers that do not support the generic transform attribute default to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.post_sample_transform"},{"category":"type","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.RawSamplerAttribute","page":"Attribute System","text":"RawSamplerAttribute{key}\n\nQUBODrivers raw optimizer attribute identified by a string-like key.\n\nRawSamplerAttribute(\"num_reads\") is the QUBODrivers counterpart of MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"num_reads\"). Generated samplers route MOI raw attributes through this type so solver-specific attributes can share the same storage and validation as typed sampler attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.RawSamplerAttribute"},{"category":"macro","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.@raw_attr_str","page":"Attribute System","text":"@raw_attr_str(\"key\")\n\nCreate a RawSamplerAttribute type for dispatch.\n\nThis macro is useful when extending validation for a particular raw attribute:\n\nfunction MOI.set(sampler::Optimizer, attr::QUBODrivers.@raw_attr_str(\"seed\"), value)\n    QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!(sampler, attr, value)\n    return nothing\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.@raw_attr_str"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.get_raw_attr","page":"Attribute System","text":"get_raw_attr(sampler, attr)\n\nReturn a raw sampler attribute value.\n\nGenerated optimizers first look in the sampler's attribute storage and then fall back to default_raw_attr. Most users access this through MOI.get(sampler, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(name)) or a typed attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.get_raw_attr"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!","page":"Attribute System","text":"set_raw_attr!(sampler, attr, value)\n\nStore a raw sampler attribute value.\n\nGenerated optimizers use this hook from MOI.set. Sampler implementations can specialize it, or specialize MOI.set for a RawSamplerAttribute, to validate backend-specific options before storing them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!"},{"category":"function","location":"booklet/3-attributes/#QUBODrivers.default_raw_attr","page":"Attribute System","text":"default_raw_attr(sampler, attr)\n\nReturn the default value for a raw sampler attribute.\n\nMethods are generated by QUBODrivers.@setup for declared attributes. Sampler implementations may add methods to customize defaults for additional raw attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.default_raw_attr"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/8-api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"This page collects the public QUBODrivers API used by solver users and sampler authors. QUBOTools provides the lower-level model and sample containers; this package documents the MOI-facing sampler layer built on top of them.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/8-api/#Package","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Package"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/8-api/#Sampler-Interface","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Sampler Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/8-api/#Setup-and-Attributes","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Setup and Attributes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/8-api/#Built-In-Samplers","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Built-In Samplers"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/8-api/#Test-and-Benchmark-Helpers","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Test and Benchmark Helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/8-api/#Re-Exported-Types","page":"API Reference","text":"QUBODrivers re-exports a few commonly used QUBOTools and MOI names:\n\nQUBODrivers.MOI aliases MathOptInterface;\nQUBODrivers.Sample and QUBODrivers.SampleSet are QUBOTools sample containers returned by QUBODrivers.sample;\nQUBODrivers.Spin, QUBODrivers.↑, and QUBODrivers.↓ are the spin domain set and values used by QUBOTools' MOI extension.","title":"Re-Exported Types"},{"category":"module","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers","page":"API Reference","text":"QUBODrivers\n\nCommon MathOptInterface-compatible tools for QUBO and Ising samplers.\n\nQUBODrivers provides:\n\nan AbstractSampler optimizer interface for QUBO-oriented solvers;\nthe @setup macro for declaring sampler optimizers and attributes;\nbuilt-in utility samplers in ExactSampler, RandomSampler, IdentitySampler, and MIPSampler;\nresult and model plumbing through MathOptInterface and QUBOTools;\noptional test and benchmark helpers for sampler implementations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractSampler{T} <: MOI.AbstractOptimizer\n\nAbstract supertype for QUBODrivers sampler optimizers.\n\nConcrete sampler optimizers are usually generated with QUBODrivers.@setup. They are MathOptInterface optimizers that accept QUBO or Ising models with binary or spin variables and return one or more sampled states through standard MOI result attributes.\n\nThe type parameter T is the numeric coefficient type used by the internal QUBOTools.Model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.sample-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"sample(::AbstractSampler{T})::SampleSet{T} where {T}\n\nRun the backend sampler and return a QUBOTools.SampleSet.\n\nSampler packages implement this method for their optimizer type. The method should read the model from the sampler, read any MOI or raw optimizer attributes it needs, call the backend, and return a SampleSet{T} whose samples use the same sense and domain as the backend output.\n\nMOI.optimize! calls this method and attaches the returned sample set to the optimizer. If the returned metadata does not include a \"time\" dictionary with a \"total\" entry, or does not include \"status\", QUBODrivers fills those fields with default values. It then validates the benchmarking metadata schema and emits a warning, not an error, for missing or malformed fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.sample"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.set_model!-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"set_model!(sampler::AbstractSampler{T}, model::QUBOTools.Model{VI,T,Int}) where {T}\n\nStore the QUBOTools model backing a sampler.\n\nThe @setup macro provides this method for generated optimizer types. Custom sampler types that do not use @setup must provide it so that MOI.copy_to can transfer JuMP/MOI models into the sampler before optimization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.set_model!"},{"category":"macro","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.@setup-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"QUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name = \"Solver Name\"\n    version = v\"1.0.0\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer = 1_000\n    end\nend\n\nDeclare a QUBODrivers sampler optimizer type.\n\nThe macro creates a mutable Optimizer{T} <: QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler{T}, QUBOTools model storage, MOI optimizer metadata, raw attribute storage, and MOI.get/MOI.set/MOI.supports methods for declared attributes.\n\nThe setup block accepts:\n\nname: required solver name returned by MOI.SolverName;\nversion: optional VersionNumber, defaulting to the QUBODrivers package version;\nattributes: optional block of solver attributes.\n\nAttributes are assignments to default values. They may be typed with ::T, exposed as typed MOI attributes, exposed as raw string attributes, or exposed as both:\n\n\"num_reads\" = 1_000\n\"num_reads\"::Integer = 1_000\nNumberOfReads = 1_000\nNumberOfReads::Integer = 1_000\nNumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"] = 1_000\nNumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer = 1_000\n\nExample\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name       = \"Super Sampler\"\n    version    = v\"1.0.2\"\n    attributes = begin\n        NumberOfReads[\"num_reads\"]::Integer  = 1_000\n        SuperAttribute[\"super_attr\"]         = nothing\n        MegaAttribute::Union{String,Nothing} = \"mega\"\n    end\nend\n\nAfter setup, implement QUBODrivers.sample for the generated optimizer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.@setup"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.SamplerAttribute-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"SamplerAttribute\n\nAbstract supertype for optimizer attributes declared by QUBODrivers samplers.\n\nAttributes generated by QUBODrivers.@setup, such as NumberOfReads, subtype SamplerAttribute and can be used with MOI.get, MOI.set, and MOI.supports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.SamplerAttribute"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.RandomSeed-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"RandomSeed()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute for a reproducibility seed.\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"seed\", and the default value is nothing. Generated optimizers support this typed attribute when the driver declares the raw \"seed\" attribute in its QUBODrivers.@setup block. When a seed is set, QUBODrivers records it under metadata[\"seeds\"][\"sampler\"] in the emitted SampleSet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.RandomSeed"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.random_seed-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"random_seed(sampler)\n\nReturn the configured sampler seed, or nothing when the sampler does not support the standard seed attribute or no seed is configured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.random_seed"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"FinalNumberOfReads()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute for the number of reads used to build the final emitted SampleSet.\n\nNumberOfReads remains the convention for reads used by a sampler's internal search, optimization, or objective evaluations. FinalNumberOfReads separates the number of reads returned to users from those internal evaluations. If the final read count is unset (nothing), the effective final read count defaults to the sampler's raw \"num_reads\" attribute when that attribute is supported.\n\nGenerated optimizers created by QUBODrivers.@setup support this attribute and the raw \"final_num_reads\" key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.final_number_of_reads-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"final_number_of_reads(sampler)\n\nReturn the effective final read count for sampler.\n\nThis is equivalent to MOI.get(sampler, FinalNumberOfReads()) for samplers generated by QUBODrivers.@setup. If \"final_num_reads\" is unset, the returned value falls back to raw \"num_reads\" when supported; otherwise it is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.final_number_of_reads"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.AutoConfigure-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"AutoConfigure()\n\nGeneric opt-in sampler optimizer attribute enabling problem-size-dependent parameter scaling (n = QUBOTools.dimension(sampler)).\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"auto_configure\", and the default value is false so existing solver behavior is unchanged unless explicitly enabled. Samplers that implement size-based scaling advertise support via supports_auto_configure, respect any explicitly user-set solver attributes over auto-scaled values (verifiable via has_explicit_raw_attr), clamp scaled parameters to explicit finite bounds, and log the chosen scaling details under metadata[\"auto_configure\"].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.AutoConfigure"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.auto_configure-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"auto_configure(sampler)::Bool\n\nReturn whether automatic problem-size parameter scaling (AutoConfigure) is enabled on sampler. Samplers that do not support the attribute default to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.auto_configure"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.supports_auto_configure-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"supports_auto_configure(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler implements automatic problem-size parameter scaling when AutoConfigure is set to true. Defaults to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.supports_auto_configure"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.has_explicit_raw_attr-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"has_explicit_raw_attr(sampler, key::Union{Symbol,AbstractString})::Bool\n\nReturn true when the raw optimizer attribute key has been explicitly set by the caller on sampler (with a non-nothing value), and false when the attribute is still at its default value. Drivers implementing AutoConfigure use this check to preserve user override precedence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.has_explicit_raw_attr"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"PostSampleCallback()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute for a callback applied after backend sampling and before the final SampleSet is attached to the optimizer.\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"post_sample_callback\". The callback is called as callback(sampleset, sampler), where sampleset is a copy of the raw backend output and sampler is the optimizer/model context. The callback may mutate the copied sample-set metadata and return nothing, or return a SampleSet to replace the emitted samples. Replacing or changing sample states, values, reads, sense, or domain requires PostSampleTransform to be set to true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.PostSampleCallback"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.PostSampleTransform-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"PostSampleTransform()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute controlling whether PostSampleCallback may transform emitted samples.\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"post_sample_transform\", and the default value is false. When set to true, a post-sample callback may return a replacement SampleSet. QUBODrivers records callback metadata and preserves the raw samples in the emitted solution metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.PostSampleTransform"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.post_sample_callback-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"post_sample_callback(sampler)\n\nReturn the configured post-sample callback for sampler, or nothing when no callback is configured or the sampler does not support the generic callback attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.post_sample_callback"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.post_sample_transform-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"post_sample_transform(sampler)::Bool\n\nReturn whether the configured post-sample callback may transform emitted sample states, values, reads, sense, or domain. Samplers that do not support the generic transform attribute default to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.post_sample_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.total_time-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"total_time(sampleset_or_sampler)\n\nReturn metadata[\"time\"][\"total\"], the wall-clock seconds measured around the QUBODrivers sampling pipeline, or nothing when it is not available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.total_time"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.effective_time-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"effective_time(sampleset_or_sampler)\n\nReturn metadata[\"time\"][\"effective\"], the backend solve/sampling seconds reported by the driver, or nothing when it is not available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.effective_time"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.validate_metadata-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"validate_metadata(sampleset::SampleSet)\nvalidate_metadata(metadata::AbstractDict)\n\nReturn a list of benchmarking metadata schema violations.\n\nThe validator is intentionally non-throwing so driver authors can use it while migrating metadata. MOI.optimize! calls it after QUBODrivers stamps framework metadata and emits a warning when the returned list is not empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.validate_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.supports_seed-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"supports_seed(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler advertises support for the standard RandomSeed attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.supports_seed"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.honors_final_reads-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"honors_final_reads(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler uses FinalNumberOfReads to control the number of samples emitted in the final SampleSet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.honors_final_reads"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.enforces_time_limit-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"enforces_time_limit(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler enforces MOI.TimeLimitSec() as part of its own sampling contract. The default is conservative because many wrappers only store or forward the attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.enforces_time_limit"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.supported_domains-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"supported_domains(sampler_or_type)\n\nReturn the collection of variable domains supported by a sampler optimizer.\n\nDefaults to Set{Symbol}([:bool, :spin]) so existing binary and spin samplers remain unchanged. Samplers that natively accept contiguous bounded integer variables (MOI.Integer with finite bounds) or explicit finite integer value sets (VariableEncodingSet) may extend this method to include :int and/or :set, for example:\n\nQUBODrivers.supported_domains(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = Set([:bool, :spin, :int, :set])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.supported_domains"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.supports_domain-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"supports_domain(sampler_or_type, domain)::Bool\n\nReturn whether sampler_or_type advertises support for domain (:bool / QUBOTools.BoolDomain, :spin / QUBOTools.SpinDomain, :int, or :set).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.supports_domain"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.VariableEncodingSet-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"VariableEncodingSet()\n\nMOI variable attribute specifying an explicit finite integer value set (x_i ∈ U_i ⊆ ℤ) for variable x_i, shared with ToQUBO.Attributes.VariableEncodingSet().\n\nWhen set on a variable in a model handed to a sampler that supports the :set domain (supports_domain(sampler, :set) == true), the variable's finite domain is Vector{Int}(U_i).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.VariableEncodingSet"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.variable_domain-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"variable_domain(sampler::AbstractSampler, vi::MOI.VariableIndex)::Vector{Int}\n\nReturn the ordered finite integer domain U_i ⊆ ℤ for variable vi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.variable_domain"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.variable_domains-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"variable_domains(sampler::AbstractSampler)::Vector{Vector{Int}}\n\nReturn the ordered finite integer domains [U_1, …, U_n] for all free variables of sampler in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.variable_domains"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.variable_bounds-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"variable_bounds(sampler::AbstractSampler, vi::MOI.VariableIndex)::Tuple{Int,Int}\n\nReturn the (lower, upper) integer bounds (minimum(U_i), maximum(U_i)) for variable vi.\n\n\n\n\n\nvariable_bounds(sampler::AbstractSampler)::Vector{Tuple{Int,Int}}\n\nReturn the (lower, upper) integer bounds for all free variables of sampler in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.variable_bounds"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.variable_num_levels-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"variable_num_levels(sampler::AbstractSampler, vi::MOI.VariableIndex)::Int\n\nReturn the number of discrete levels length(U_i) in the finite domain of variable vi.\n\n\n\n\n\nvariable_num_levels(sampler::AbstractSampler)::Vector{Int}\n\nReturn the number of discrete levels for each free variable of sampler in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.variable_num_levels"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.levels_to_values-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"levels_to_values(sampler::AbstractSampler, vi::MOI.VariableIndex, level::Integer)::Int\nlevels_to_values(sampler::AbstractSampler, levels::AbstractVector{<:Integer})::Vector{Int}\n\nMap zero-based level indices k_i ∈ 0:(d_i - 1) to variable domain values U_i[k_i + 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.levels_to_values"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.values_to_levels-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"values_to_levels(sampler::AbstractSampler, vi::MOI.VariableIndex, value::Real)::Int\nvalues_to_levels(sampler::AbstractSampler, values::AbstractVector{<:Real})::Vector{Int}\n\nInvert variable domain values v_i ∈ U_i back to zero-based level indices k_i ∈ 0:(d_i - 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.values_to_levels"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.supports_polynomial_objective-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"supports_polynomial_objective(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler opts into receiving general polynomial objectives (including higher-order HUBO/PUBO objectives expressed via MOI.ScalarNonlinearFunction or ScalarPolynomialFunction).\n\nDefaults to false so quadratic QUBO/Ising samplers remain on the standard quadratic lowering path and reject degree >= 3 objectives as before.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.supports_polynomial_objective"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.polynomial_objective-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"polynomial_objective(\n    sampler::AbstractSampler{T};\n    sense::Union{Symbol,QUBOTools.Sense,Nothing} = nothing,\n    domain::Union{Symbol,QUBOTools.Domain,Nothing} = nothing,\n    shift::Bool = false,\n)::MP.AbstractPolynomial{T} where {T}\n\nReturn the objective polynomial stored on an opted-in sampler as a MultivariatePolynomials.AbstractPolynomial{T} over the free variables in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order, preserving constant offset and fixed variable reductions.\n\nIf sense (:min or :max) or domain (:bool or :spin) is provided, the polynomial is cast to the requested optimization sense and variable domain. When shift = true, contiguous unit-step integer domains {l_i, …, u_i} are shifted via x_i mapsto y_i + l_i to zero-based level coordinates y_i in 0 dots u_i - l_i.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError if neither supports_polynomial_objective(sampler) nor non-binary domain support is enabled on sampler.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.polynomial_objective"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.polynomial_function-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"polynomial_function(p::MP.AbstractPolynomial{T}, variables::Vector{MOI.VariableIndex}) where {T}\npolynomial_function(func::MOI.AbstractScalarFunction, ::Type{T} = Float64) where {T}\npolynomial_function(sampler::AbstractSampler{T}; sense = nothing, domain = nothing) where {T}\n\nConstruct or extract a ScalarPolynomialFunction representing a scalar polynomial objective or constraint expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.polynomial_function"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.polynomial_type-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"polynomial_type(::Type{MOI.VariableIndex})\npolynomial_type(::Type{MOI.VariableIndex}, ::Type{T}) where {T}\npolynomial_type(::Type{T} = Float64) where {T<:Number}\n\nReturn the concrete DynamicPolynomials.Polynomial type used by QUBODrivers to represent MOI polynomial functions with coefficient type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.polynomial_type"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.ScalarPolynomialFunction-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"struct ScalarPolynomialFunction{T,P<:MP.AbstractPolynomial{T}} <: MOI.AbstractScalarFunction\n    polynomial::P\n    variables::Vector{MOI.VariableIndex}\nend\n\nDefines the polynomial function of the variables variables where the variable MultivariatePolynomials.variables(polynomial)[i] corresponds to variables[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.ScalarPolynomialFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.ToPolynomialBridge-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"ToPolynomialBridge{T}\n\nToPolynomialBridge implements the following reformulations:\n\nmin f(x) into minp(x)\nmax f(x) into maxp(x)\n\nwhere f(x) is a scalar function and p(x) is a polynomial.\n\nSource node\n\nToPolynomialBridge supports:\n\nMOI.ObjectiveFunction{F} where F is a MOI.AbstractScalarFunction for which convert(::Type{QUBODrivers.ScalarPolynomialFunction{T,P}}, ::F) is defined, such as MOI.VariableIndex, MOI.ScalarAffineFunction{T}, MOI.ScalarQuadraticFunction{T}, and MOI.ScalarNonlinearFunction.\n\nTarget nodes\n\nToPolynomialBridge creates:\n\nOne objective node: MOI.ObjectiveFunction{QUBODrivers.ScalarPolynomialFunction{T,P}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.ToPolynomialBridge"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.NLToPolynomial-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"NLToPolynomial{T,M<:MOI.ModelLike} <: MOI.AbstractOptimizer\n\nOptimizer wrapper that rewrites nonlinear polynomial expressions into ScalarPolynomialFunction objectives and constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.NLToPolynomial"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.InvalidNLExpression-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"InvalidNLExpression <: Exception\n\nException thrown when a nonlinear expression cannot be converted into a polynomial (for example, when it uses a non-polynomial operator such as sin, non-constant division /, or a non-integer or negative exponent in ^).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.InvalidNLExpression"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.RawSamplerAttribute-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"RawSamplerAttribute{key}\n\nQUBODrivers raw optimizer attribute identified by a string-like key.\n\nRawSamplerAttribute(\"num_reads\") is the QUBODrivers counterpart of MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"num_reads\"). Generated samplers route MOI raw attributes through this type so solver-specific attributes can share the same storage and validation as typed sampler attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.RawSamplerAttribute"},{"category":"macro","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.@raw_attr_str-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"@raw_attr_str(\"key\")\n\nCreate a RawSamplerAttribute type for dispatch.\n\nThis macro is useful when extending validation for a particular raw attribute:\n\nfunction MOI.set(sampler::Optimizer, attr::QUBODrivers.@raw_attr_str(\"seed\"), value)\n    QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!(sampler, attr, value)\n    return nothing\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.@raw_attr_str"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.get_raw_attr-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"get_raw_attr(sampler, attr)\n\nReturn a raw sampler attribute value.\n\nGenerated optimizers first look in the sampler's attribute storage and then fall back to default_raw_attr. Most users access this through MOI.get(sampler, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(name)) or a typed attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.get_raw_attr"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"set_raw_attr!(sampler, attr, value)\n\nStore a raw sampler attribute value.\n\nGenerated optimizers use this hook from MOI.set. Sampler implementations can specialize it, or specialize MOI.set for a RawSamplerAttribute, to validate backend-specific options before storing them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.set_raw_attr!"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.default_raw_attr-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"default_raw_attr(sampler, attr)\n\nReturn the default value for a raw sampler attribute.\n\nMethods are generated by QUBODrivers.@setup for declared attributes. Sampler implementations may add methods to customize defaults for additional raw attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.default_raw_attr"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.ExactSampler.Optimizer-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"ExactSampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nThis sampler performs an exhaustive search over all 2^n possible states. It is useful as a correctness oracle for small QUBO and Ising models.\n\nwarn: Warn\nDue to the exponentially large number of visited states, this sampler is intended only for small instances.\n\nExactSampler ignores QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads; it always returns the full exhaustive sample set. A completed solve exposes MOI.OPTIMAL through MOI.TerminationStatus(). Post-sample transformations do not retain that certificate; annotation-only callbacks preserve it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.ExactSampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.RandomSampler.Optimizer-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"RandomSampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nSampler that evaluates uniformly random states.\n\nRandomSampler is a lightweight baseline for smoke tests, examples, and benchmark harnesses. It samples independent states in the model domain, evaluates their objective values with QUBOTools, and returns the final sampled states.\n\nAttributes\n\nQUBODrivers.RandomSeed, \"seed\": Random seed to initialize the random number generator.\nNumberOfReads, \"num_reads\": Default final read count.\nQUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads, \"final_num_reads\": Number of random states emitted in the returned sample set. If unset, this defaults to NumberOfReads.\nRandomGenerator, \"rng\": Random Number Generator instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.RandomSampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.IdentitySampler.Optimizer-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"IdentitySampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nThis sampler selects precisely the state vector provided as warm-start. Use it to check model conversion, objective evaluation, fixed variables, and warm-start plumbing without invoking a stochastic or external backend.\n\nEvery variable must have a valid MOI.VariablePrimalStart value before optimization.\n\nIdentitySampler ignores QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads; it always returns the single warm-start state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.IdentitySampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.Optimizer-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"MIPSampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nMOI-native exact baseline that linearizes a QUBO into a binary mixed-integer linear model and solves it with a user-supplied MOI optimizer.\n\nMIPSampler does not depend on any concrete MIP solver. Set MIPOptimizer to a compatible optimizer factory, such as HiGHS.Optimizer, GLPK.Optimizer, or MOI.OptimizerWithAttributes(HiGHS.Optimizer, MOI.Silent() => true).\n\nThe sampler introduces one binary auxiliary variable for each off-diagonal quadratic term and enforces the product relation with the standard Fortet/McCormick inequalities. This follows the usual linear reformulation route for binary polynomial optimization; see also Elloumi, Sourour, and Verchere, \"Efficient linear reformulations for binary polynomial optimization problems\", Computers & Operations Research 155 (2023), 106240.\n\nAt most one best incumbent sample is returned, with its binary state rounded and independently re-evaluated on the full QUBO objective. MIPSampler ignores QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads.\n\nObjective Bounds, Gaps, and Limit Terminations\n\nObjective bounds and gaps (MOI.ObjectiveBound, MOI.RelativeGap): When the MIP backend reports a finite objective bound, MIPSampler translates it back to the caller's original objective sense (MOI.MIN_SENSE or MOI.MAX_SENSE), variable domain (MOI.ZeroOne or QUBODrivers.Spin), scaling, constant offset, and fixed-variable (MOI.EqualTo) conditioning, and exposes it via MOI.ObjectiveBound() as well as metadata[\"objective_bound\"] and metadata[\"bound\"]. If no finite objective bound is available for the current solve state, MOI.get(model, MOI.ObjectiveBound()) throws MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed{MOI.ObjectiveBound}.\nScope: The bound applies to the supplied QUBO/Ising objective, possibly conditioned on any fixed variables in the model. A bound on a penalized QUBO relaxation is not automatically a valid bound on an original constrained problem unless the penalty parameter has been independently certified.\nNumerical provenance: Bounds and relative gaps are backend-reported, tolerance-dependent quantities, not exact-arithmetic certificates. Do not treat them as exact proofs in certified preprocessing without a separate verification layer, and do not infer global optimality from a small observed gap when MOI.TerminationStatus() is not MOI.OPTIMAL. Effective gap tolerances in metadata[\"bound\"] are recorded only when confirmed by backend readback or attribute forwarding; requested sampler settings are stored separately under requested_relative_gap_tolerance and requested_absolute_gap_tolerance.\nRelative gap: When an incumbent and a bound are both present and the backend reports a finite MOI.RelativeGap(), MIPSampler exposes that solver-dependent value through MOI.RelativeGap() and metadata[\"relative_gap\"] (labeled \"backend_solver_dependent\" in metadata[\"bound\"][\"relative_gap_definition\"]). It is not interchangeable with a gap recomputed from the rounded incumbent, and when no finite gap is available (including primal-free results or backends that return NaN/Inf), MOI.get(model, MOI.RelativeGap()) throws MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed{MOI.RelativeGap}.\nPrimal-free limit terminations vs. failures: If the backend stops on a normal limit or interruption (MOI.TIME_LIMIT, MOI.NODE_LIMIT, MOI.ITERATION_LIMIT, MOI.SOLUTION_LIMIT, MOI.MEMORY_LIMIT, MOI.OBJECTIVE_LIMIT, MOI.NORM_LIMIT, MOI.OTHER_LIMIT, or MOI.INTERRUPTED) without a feasible incumbent, MIPSampler returns an empty SampleSet (MOI.ResultCount() == 0, MOI.PrimalStatus() == MOI.NO_SOLUTION) while preserving MOI.TerminationStatus(), MOI.ObjectiveBound() (when available), and solve timing metadata. Conversely, numerical validation failures (NaN or unrepresentable bounds/objectives/gaps, inconsistent bound > incumbent, invalid primal values) and backend solve errors throw an exception from MOI.optimize!, clear any prior results (MOI.ResultCount() == 0, MOI.PrimalStatus() == MOI.NO_SOLUTION), and set MOI.TerminationStatus() to MOI.NUMERICAL_ERROR or MOI.OTHER_ERROR (or the backend's failure status).\n\nAttributes\n\nMIPOptimizer, \"mip_optimizer\": MOI-compatible optimizer factory used to solve the generated binary MIP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.MIPOptimizer-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"MIPOptimizer()\n\nOptimizer attribute that stores the MOI-compatible MIP backend factory used by MIPSampler.Optimizer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.MIPOptimizer"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.test-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"test(optimizer::Type{S}; examples::Bool=true, kwargs...) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\ntest(config!::Function, optimizer::Type{S}; examples::Bool=true, kwargs...) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\n\nRun QUBODrivers' interface and example test suite for a sampler implementation.\n\nThese methods are implemented by the QUBODrivers_Test_Ext package extension and become available when Test is loaded in the active environment. The core package does not depend on Test directly.\n\nBenchmark conformance checks are enabled by default. Pass benchmark_conformance=false to disable all benchmark conformance checks, or disable individual groups with metadata_conformance=false, timing_sanity=false, determinism=false, reads_semantics=false, time_limit_acceptance=false, or termination_status=false. The default determinism=:auto runs seed determinism only for samplers whose supports_seed trait is true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.test"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/8-api/#QUBODrivers.benchmark-manual-8-api","page":"API Reference","text":"benchmark(optimizer::Type{S}; objective_sense=MOI.MIN_SENSE) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\nbenchmark(config!::Function, optimizer::Type{S}; objective_sense=MOI.MIN_SENSE) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\nbenchmark(optimizer::Type{S}, source::MOI.ModelLike) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\nbenchmark(config!::Function, optimizer::Type{S}, source::MOI.ModelLike) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\n\nRun a QUBO benchmark problem with a sampler implementation.\n\nWhen source is provided, benchmark the sampler on that MOI model. The no-source methods use a small built-in QUBO instance as a smoke benchmark.\n\nThe optional config! function receives the raw sampler optimizer after the source model is copied and before optimization. This is useful for setting sampler attributes such as seeds, number of reads, time limits, or warm-starts.\n\nThe return value is a named tuple with solver metadata, benchmark problem size, result count, objective values, read counts, objective-value summary statistics, the best result, MOI solve time, measured wall time around MOI.optimize!, and solver status fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.benchmark"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-solve/#Solving-QUBO","page":"Solving QUBO","text":"QUBODrivers samplers are MathOptInterface optimizers, so the usual JuMP workflow applies: choose an optimizer, build a binary or spin model, call optimize!, and query results with JuMP or MOI.","title":"Solving QUBO"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-solve/#Build-and-Solve-a-Binary-QUBO","page":"Solving QUBO","text":"The built-in ExactSampler.Optimizer is deterministic and enumerates all states, making it useful for small examples and correctness checks.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(ExactSampler.Optimizer)\n\nQ = [\n    -1.0  2.0  2.0\n     2.0 -1.0  2.0\n     2.0  2.0 -1.0\n]\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, x' * Q * x)\n\noptimize!(model)","title":"Build and Solve a Binary QUBO"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-solve/#Query-Results","page":"Solving QUBO","text":"Samplers may return more than one result. Use result_count(model) to iterate through the available states.\n\nsolutions = [\n    (round.(Int, value.(x; result = i)), objective_value(model; result = i))\n    for i in 1:result_count(model)\n]\n\nminimum(last, solutions)\n-1.0\n\nThe same results are available through MOI:\n\nMOI = QUBODrivers.MOI\n\nMOI.get(backend(model), MOI.ResultCount())","title":"Query Results"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-solve/#Set-Sampler-Attributes","page":"Solving QUBO","text":"Sampler options can be set with standard MOI raw optimizer attributes or JuMP's set_optimizer_attribute.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(RandomSampler.Optimizer)\nset_optimizer_attribute(model, \"num_reads\", 10)\nset_optimizer_attribute(model, \"seed\", 123)\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, -x[1] - x[2] - x[3] + 2x[1] * x[2])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nresult_count(model)\n6\n\nTyped attributes generated by QUBODrivers.@setup can also be used directly through MOI:\n\noptimizer = RandomSampler.Optimizer()\nMOI.set(optimizer, RandomSampler.NumberOfReads(), 10)\nMOI.set(optimizer, QUBODrivers.RandomSeed(), 123)","title":"Set Sampler Attributes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-solve/#Fixed-Variables-and-Starts","page":"Solving QUBO","text":"QUBODrivers supports binary variables, spin variables, fixed variable constraints, and MOI.VariablePrimalStart. In JuMP, fixed variables can be created with fix.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(ExactSampler.Optimizer)\n\n@variable(model, x[1:2], Bin)\nfix(x[1], 1; force = true)\n@objective(model, Min, -x[1] + x[1] * x[2])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nround.(Int, value.(x; result = 1))\n2-element Vector{Int64}:\n 1\n 0\n\nUse IdentitySampler.Optimizer when you want the sampler result to be exactly the provided warm-start state. Set starts through MOI.VariablePrimalStart before optimization:\n\nMOI.set(model, MOI.VariablePrimalStart(), variable, start_value)","title":"Fixed Variables and Starts"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/2-solve/#Supported-Model-Shape","page":"Solving QUBO","text":"QUBODrivers samplers accept unconstrained binary or spin models with linear, quadratic, or single-variable objectives. MOI bridges may reformulate some JuMP input, but sampler backends ultimately receive a QUBOTools model in one domain:\n\nboolean variables in 0 1;\nspin variables in -1 1;\nminimization or maximization objective sense;\noptional fixed variables folded into the internal model before sampling.","title":"Supported Model Shape"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Optional-TKET-/-Quantinuum-Nexus-Sampler-Design","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"This page records the contract and package-boundary design for an optional TKET and Quantinuum Nexus Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) sampler wrapper for the JuliaQUBO ecosystem (JuliaQUBO/QUBODrivers.jl#89). It specifies package ownership, the separation between credential-free local pytket execution and remote Quantinuum Nexus job execution, logical-to-physical circuit bit mapping, persistent Nexus job recovery without duplicate paid submissions, and the local conformance and mocked lifecycle test plan.\n\nnote: Design Status and Package Boundary\nQUBODrivers.jl hosts this interface specification and conformance checklist only; it does not import pytket or qnexus, and no TKET or Quantinuum SDK dependencies are added to QUBODrivers.jl, QUBOTools.jl, ToQUBO.jl, QUBO.jl, or QUBODecomposition.jl. Once the standalone repository is bootstrapped and passes conformance, it will be added to the External Sampler Packages table in docs/src/manual/3-samplers.md.","title":"Optional TKET / Quantinuum Nexus Sampler Design"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Ownership-and-Package-Placement","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"Proposed package and module: A standalone optional wrapper package, TKETOpt.jl (hosted under JuliaQUBO or SECQUOIA), exposing TKETOpt.QAOA.Optimizer{T} <: QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler{T} generated via QUBODrivers.@setup.\nZero vendor leakage into core packages: QUBODrivers.jl and QUBODecomposition.jl interact with TKETOpt.QAOA.Optimizer exclusively through the public QUBODrivers.AbstractSampler and MathOptInterface contracts (see Public composition pattern).\nIsolated Python environment: pytket, local simulator backends (such as pytket-qiskit or pytket state/shot simulators), and qnexus have distinct version and dependency constraints from QiskitOpt.jl and IQM clients. The standalone package must document an isolated CondaPkg / PythonCall environment so users can install TKETOpt.jl without conflicting with QiskitOpt.jl.\nUpstream reference attribution: Where QAOA circuit construction, local TKET compilation, or Nexus submission adapts patterns from alpha-unito/QSplit (tket_qaoa_cpu_noiseless.py, tket_qaoa_nexus.py, and util.py), the wrapper repository must preserve the QSplit MIT license notice and record the adapted revision (4da64b072e702953038addd51cdf54f97f0f9516).","title":"Ownership and Package Placement"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Minimum-Sampler-Surface-and-Execution-Modes","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"TKETOpt.QAOA.Optimizer must explicitly separate local credential-free TKET simulation from remote Quantinuum Nexus emulator and hardware execution rather than assuming IBM Qiskit primitive compatibility:\n\nAttribute / Raw Key Type & Default Contract\n\"execution_mode\" String = \"local_tket\" Explicit execution route: \"local_tket\" (credential-free local pytket backend), \"nexus_emulator\" (remote Quantinuum Nexus emulator), or \"nexus_hardware\" (remote Quantinuum QPU).\n\"backend_name\" String = \"AerBackend\" Target backend identifier (for example \"AerBackend\" in \"local_tket\" mode, or \"H1-1E\" / \"H1-1\" in Nexus modes).\n\"p\" Integer = 1 QAOA circuit depth (p ge 1), parameterizing 2p angles (gamma_1 dots gamma_p beta_1 dots beta_p).\n\"optimisation_level\" Integer = 2 TKET compiler optimization level (0, 1, 2, or 3) passed to backend.get_compiled_circuit or qnexus.start_compile_job.\n\"num_reads\" Integer = 1_024 Shot count (n_shots) per circuit evaluation during variational parameter optimization.\nQUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads() / \"final_num_reads\" Union{Integer,Nothing} = nothing Shot count (n_shots) used for final sample-set generation (honors_final_reads(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = true; defaults to \"num_reads\" when unset).\n\"optimizer\" String = \"COBYLA\" Classical outer-loop optimizer name.\n\"max_iterations\" Integer = 100 Maximum classical optimizer iterations (max_iterations >= 1).\n\"initial_point\" Union{Vector{Float64},Nothing} = nothing Optional length-2p initial parameter vector; when nothing, initialized deterministically from \"seed\".\nQUBODrivers.RandomSeed() / \"seed\" Union{Integer,Nothing} = nothing Seed forwarded to local backend shot sampling (seed keyword in process_circuits / get_result) and classical optimizer initialization (supports_seed(::Type{<:Optimizer}) = true).\n\"nexus_project\" Union{String,Nothing} = nothing Explicit Quantinuum Nexus project name or reference (required for \"nexus_emulator\" and \"nexus_hardware\" modes).\n\"job_persistence_path\" Union{String,Nothing} = nothing Optional JSON file path where accepted Nexus compile/execute job descriptors are persisted immediately upon submission.\n\"nexus_resume_job_id\" Union{String,Nothing} = nothing Optional existing Nexus execute job ID to inspect/retrieve before submitting any new remote compilation or execution job.\n\"poll_timeout_sec\" Float64 = 900.0 Maximum wall-clock seconds to wait for a submitted Nexus compile or execute job before timing out.\n\"poll_interval_sec\" Float64 = 5.0 Polling interval in seconds when checking Nexus job status.\n\"cancel_on_timeout\" Bool = false Whether to issue a best-effort cancellation request (qnexus.jobs.cancel) when polling exceeds \"poll_timeout_sec\".","title":"Minimum Sampler Surface and Execution Modes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Model-Lowering,-Compiled-Circuit-Bit-Mapping,-and-Scoring","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"Logical variable order and QUBOTools spin convention: Retrieve the internal model in QUBOTools.variables(sampler) order (1:n) via n, h, J, α, β = QUBOTools.ising(sampler, :dict; sense = :min). In QUBOTools.jl, BoolDomain (x_i in 0 1) and SpinDomain (s_i in -1 +1) are related by s_i = 2x_i - 1 (x_i = (1 + s_i)2, so x_i = 0 leftrightarrow s_i = -1 and x_i = 1 leftrightarrow s_i = +1). Under computational-basis bit encoding x_irangle (b_i = 0 leftrightarrow x_i = 0, b_i = 1 leftrightarrow x_i = 1), the Pauli-Z operator satisfies Z_i-1x_irangle = (1 - 2x_i)x_irangle = -s_ix_irangle. Substituting s_i = -Z_i-1 into the QUBOTools.ising form (equivalently substituting x_i = (I - Z_i-1)2 into QUBOTools.qubo) gives the Pauli-Z Cost Hamiltonian:\nH_C = -sum_i=1^n h_i Z_i-1 + sum_1 le i  j le n J_ij Z_i-1 Z_j-1\nTKET half-turn (pi-radian) gate parameterization and isolated qubits: Allocate Circuit(n, n) with logical qubits Qubit(0) through Qubit(n - 1) and classical readout bits Bit(0) through Bit(n - 1).\nInitialize every qubit i in 1n (including isolated variables with zero linear and quadratic terms) with H(i - 1).\nBecause pytket rotation gates (Rz, Rx, ZZPhase) take angles in half-turns (multiples of pi radians, where textRz(alpha) = e^-i fracpi2 alpha Z and textZZPhase(alpha) = e^-i fracpi2 alpha (Z otimes Z)), layer l in 1p applies:\nRz(-2 * γ_l * h_i / π, i - 1) for each nonzero linear term h_i,\nZZPhase(2 * γ_l * J_{ij} / π, i - 1, j - 1) for each nonzero quadratic term J_ij (1 le i  j le n), and\nmixer Rx(2 * β_l / π, i - 1) on every qubit i in 1n.\nAppend Measure(Qubit(i - 1), Bit(i - 1)) for every i in 1n.\nPreserving logical variable order across TKET compilation: TKET compilation passes (backend.get_compiled_circuit or qnexus.start_compile_job) may relabel or permute physical qubits. To guarantee that readout bit k - 1 always corresponds to logical variable QUBOTools.variables(sampler)[k]:\nAlways request shot counts explicitly ordered by the logical classical register bits:\nlogical_cbits = [Bit(i) for i in range(n)]\ncounts = backend_result.get_counts(cbits=logical_cbits)\nFor each (bit_tuple, count) in counts.items():\nWhen constructing a :bool SampleSet (domain = :bool), map x_k = Int(bit_tuple[k]) (0 or 1) and score with lambda = textQUBOToolsvalue(x L Q alpha_q beta_q) from QUBOTools.qubo(sampler, :dict; sense = :min).\nWhen constructing a :spin SampleSet (domain = :spin), map s_k = (Int(bit_tuple[k]) == 1 ? 1 : -1) and score with lambda = textQUBOToolsvalue(s h J alpha beta) from QUBOTools.ising(sampler, :dict; sense = :min).\nEmit QUBOTools.Sample{T,Int}(ψ, λ, Int(count)) so shot multiplicities round-trip as sample read counts (sum(counts) == final_num_reads).","title":"Model Lowering, Compiled-Circuit Bit Mapping, and Scoring"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Nexus-Job-Lifecycle,-Persistence,-and-Non-Resubmission-Contract","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"Remote execution in \"nexus_emulator\" and \"nexus_hardware\" modes uses asynchronous compilation and execution jobs (qnexus.start_compile_job, qnexus.start_execute_job). Because Quantinuum hardware and cloud emulator credits are metered, the wrapper must enforce strict job-lifecycle safety:\n\nPersist job identity before polling: Immediately after start_compile_job or start_execute_job returns an accepted job handle, record its \"compile_job_id\" / \"execute_job_id\", \"backend_name\", \"nexus_project\", \"submitted_at\", and logical variable order in memory and—when \"job_persistence_path\" is configured—atomically write the job ledger to disk before entering any polling wait loop.\nInspect/recover before retry (no silent resubmission):\nIf \"nexus_resume_job_id\" is provided (or an in-flight job entry already exists in \"job_persistence_path\" for the active solve), query qnexus.jobs.get(id=job_id) and qnexus.jobs.status(job) first:\nIf the job is COMPLETED, retrieve its BackendResult via qnexus.jobs.results(job) without submitting a new job.\nIf the job is QUEUED, RUNNING, or SUBMITTED, resume polling that existing job up to \"poll_timeout_sec\".\nIf the job is FAILED, CANCELLED, or DEPLETED, do not silently submit a replacement job; surface the remote status and error details.\nIf a network error occurs during submission itself and it is unknown whether Nexus accepted the job, query recent project jobs matching the run's unique client job name/tag before attempting any retry.\nTimeout and cancellation truth:\nWhen polling exceeds \"poll_timeout_sec\", if \"cancel_on_timeout\" = true, call qnexus.jobs.cancel(job) and record \"cancel_requested\" = true.\nNever equate a cancellation request with confirmed cancellation: re-query qnexus.jobs.status(job) and set \"cancelled\" = true only when the remote status is confirmed as CANCELLED.\nOn timeout or remote failure, clear any stale sample results from previous solves, set MOI.TerminationStatus() to MOI.TIME_LIMIT (on poll timeout) or MOI.OTHER_ERROR (on remote job failure), preserve the job ledger in metadata[\"tket\"][\"nexus_jobs\"], and throw an exception containing the execute_job_id so the user can resume retrieval later.","title":"Nexus Job Lifecycle, Persistence, and Non-Resubmission Contract"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Metadata-Contract","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"Every completed solve must pass QUBODrivers.validate_metadata with zero violations and populate:\n\n\"origin\": \"TKETOpt.QAOA @ pytket\" (or \"TKETOpt.QAOA @ qnexus\")\n\"algorithm\": Dict(\"name\" => \"QAOA\", \"p\" => p, \"optimizer\" => optimizer_name)\n\"backend\": Dict(\"name\" => backend_name, \"version\" => pytket_version)\n\"execution\": Dict(\"mode\" => execution_mode) (\"local_tket\", \"nexus_emulator\", or \"nexus_hardware\")\n\"optimizer\": Dict(\"iterations\" => iterations_performed, \"evaluations\" => circuit_evaluations)\n\"reads\": Dict(\"number_of_reads\" => num_reads, \"final_number_of_reads\" => final_num_reads)\n\"seeds\": Dict(\"sampler\" => seed) when seeded\n\"tket\":\nDict{String,Any}(\n    \"execution_mode\"     => execution_mode,\n    \"backend_name\"       => backend_name,\n    \"optimisation_level\" => optimisation_level,\n    \"num_qubits\"         => n,\n    \"nexus_project\"      => nexus_project,\n    \"nexus_jobs\"         => nexus_job_records, # compile/execute IDs, statuses, cancel flags\n)","title":"Metadata Contract"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Local-Backend-Conformance-and-Mocked-Lifecycle-Test-Plan","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"","title":"Local Backend Conformance and Mocked Lifecycle Test Plan"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Supported-Python-/-TKET-Environment-Probe","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"using PythonCall\n\nfunction probe_tket_environment()\n    sys = pyimport(\"sys\")\n    py_version = pyconvert(String, sys.version)\n    pytket = try\n        pyimport(\"pytket\")\n    catch err\n        error(\n            \"Python package `pytket` is not installed in the active PythonCall environment \" *\n            \"(Python $py_version). Install `pytket >= 1.30` and a local simulator plugin \" *\n            \"(such as `pytket-qiskit`) before running TKETOpt.\",\n        )\n    end\n\n    pytket_version = hasproperty(pytket, :__version__) ?\n        pyconvert(String, pytket.__version__) : \"unknown\"\n    has_aer = try\n        pyimport(\"pytket.extensions.qiskit\")\n        true\n    catch\n        false\n    end\n    has_qnexus = try\n        pyimport(\"qnexus\")\n        true\n    catch\n        false\n    end\n\n    return (\n        python_version = py_version,\n        pytket_version = pytket_version,\n        has_aer        = has_aer,\n        has_qnexus     = has_qnexus,\n    )\nend","title":"Supported Python / TKET Environment Probe"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/tket-nexus-design/#Verification-Suite-for-the-Standalone-Package","page":"TKET / Nexus Sampler Design","text":"Credential-free local TKET conformance (\"execution_mode\" => \"local_tket\"):\nRun QUBODrivers.test(TKETOpt.QAOA.Optimizer; examples = true, benchmark_conformance = true) using a local simulator backend (AerBackend), \"p\" => 1, \"num_reads\" => 64, \"max_iterations\" => 5, and \"seed\" => 42.\nVerify a QUBO with reordered MOI.VariableIndex IDs, non-zero scale and constant offset, isolated variables, fixed variables, and non-uniform shot multiplicities, independently checking every returned sample's energy against QUBOTools.value and QUBODrivers.ExactSampler.Optimizer.\nVerify that get_counts(cbits = [Bit(0), ..., Bit(n - 1)]) preserves the exact logical variable order even when a synthetic qubit permutation pass is applied during compilation.\nDeterministic mocked Nexus job-lifecycle tests (no cloud credentials):\nInject a mock qnexus client to simulate:\nnormal compile to execute to poll to get_counts completion;\npoll timeout after accepted job submission (MOI.TIME_LIMIT, job ID persisted to \"job_persistence_path\", no duplicate submission);\ninterrupted persistence / polling error followed by resumption via \"nexus_resume_job_id\", verifying that the existing accepted job is reused and start_execute_job is not called a second time;\ntimeout with \"cancel_on_timeout\" = true where qnexus.jobs.cancel is requested, distinguishing \"cancel_requested\" == true and \"cancelled\" == false (when the backend remains RUNNING) from confirmed \"cancelled\" == true (when the backend transitions to CANCELLED);\nremote FAILED status clearing prior results and setting MOI.TerminationStatus() == MOI.OTHER_ERROR.\nDecomposition child integration test:\nVerify that TKETOpt.QAOA.Optimizer composes as the child factory of QUBODecomposition.Optimizer in \"local_tket\" mode using only the generic driver contract.","title":"Verification Suite for the Standalone Package"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/6-benchmarks/#Benchmarking","page":"Benchmarking","text":"Benchmarking QUBO samplers is partly about runtime and partly about solution quality. QUBODrivers exposes both through standard JuMP/MOI result queries, so a simple benchmark can collect elapsed time, number of returned states, objective values, read counts, objective-value frequencies, and the best state found.","title":"Benchmarking"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/6-benchmarks/#Benchmark-a-JuMP-Model","page":"Benchmarking","text":"Build the problem once, then pass its MOI backend to QUBODrivers.benchmark. The benchmark helper copies that model into each sampler, applies any sampler configuration, runs MOI.optimize!, and returns timing and sample-quality metadata.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model()\n@variable(model, x[1:4], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, -x[1] - 2x[2] - x[3] + 2x[1] * x[2] + x[3] * x[4])\n\nresult = QUBODrivers.benchmark(RandomSampler.Optimizer, backend(model)) do sampler\n    MOI.set(sampler, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"num_reads\"), 100)\n    MOI.set(sampler, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"seed\"), 1)\nend\n\n(result.variables, result.result_count, result.objective_summary.minimum)\n(4, 16, -3.0)\n\nFor large models, use stochastic samplers such as RandomSampler as the cheap baseline. ExactSampler is useful for small reference instances and smoke tests, but its exhaustive enumeration grows exponentially.","title":"Benchmark a JuMP Model"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/6-benchmarks/#Comparing-Samplers","page":"Benchmarking","text":"The built-in utility samplers are useful baselines. ExactSampler gives a complete reference on small instances, while RandomSampler provides a cheap stochastic baseline.\n\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nsource = MOI.Utilities.Model{Float64}()\nx, _ = MOI.add_constrained_variables(source, fill(MOI.ZeroOne(), 3))\n\nQ = [\n    -1.0  2.0  2.0\n     2.0 -1.0  2.0\n     2.0  2.0 -1.0\n]\n\nMOI.set(source, MOI.ObjectiveSense(), MOI.MIN_SENSE)\nMOI.set(\n    source,\n    MOI.ObjectiveFunction{MOI.ScalarQuadraticFunction{Float64}}(),\n    MOI.ScalarQuadraticFunction{Float64}(\n        MOI.ScalarQuadraticTerm{Float64}[\n            MOI.ScalarQuadraticTerm{Float64}(Q[i, j], x[i], x[j])\n            for i = 1:3 for j = 1:3 if i != j\n        ],\n        MOI.ScalarAffineTerm{Float64}[\n            MOI.ScalarAffineTerm{Float64}(Q[i, i], x[i])\n            for i = 1:3\n        ],\n        0.0,\n    ),\n)\n\nexact = QUBODrivers.benchmark(ExactSampler.Optimizer, source)\n\nrandom = QUBODrivers.benchmark(RandomSampler.Optimizer, source) do sampler\n    MOI.set(sampler, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"num_reads\"), 100)\n    MOI.set(sampler, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"seed\"), 1)\nend\n\n(exact.best_objective, random.best_objective)\n(-1.0, -1.0)\n\nQUBODrivers.benchmark copies a user-provided MOI model into the sampler and returns a named tuple with solver metadata, objective values, result count, best state, best objective value, read counts, objective summary, MOI solve time, wall-clock time around MOI.optimize!, and status fields. result.objective_summary contains minimum, maximum, a read-weighted mean, total_reads, and a histogram mapping each observed objective value to its total read count. The optional do block receives the raw sampler optimizer after model copy and before optimization. If no model is provided, QUBODrivers.benchmark falls back to a small built-in QUBO instance as a smoke benchmark.","title":"Comparing Samplers"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/6-benchmarks/#Timing-Information","page":"Benchmarking","text":"Samplers store timing metadata in the returned sample set. The standardized entries are metadata[\"time\"][\"total\"], the QUBODrivers wall-clock time around the sampling pipeline, and metadata[\"time\"][\"effective\"], the backend solve/sampling time reported by the driver.\n\nAfter solving, MOI.get(backend(model), MOI.SolveTimeSec()) returns effective time. Use QUBODrivers.total_time(sampleset) or QUBODrivers.total_time(sampler) when the benchmark needs framework-inclusive wall time. This can differ from timing around optimize!, especially for wrappers that separate model conversion, backend submission, queue time, and sample decoding. See the Metadata Schema for the full contract.","title":"Timing Information"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/6-benchmarks/#What-to-Report","page":"Benchmarking","text":"For stochastic or hardware-backed samplers, report enough context for the result to be reproducible:\n\npackage and backend versions;\nproblem size and density;\nobjective sense and variable domain;\nsampler attributes, especially seeds, number of reads, time limits, and threads;\nbest objective value, distribution of objective values, and success rate if a reference optimum is known;\nwall-clock time and solver-reported timing metadata.","title":"What to Report"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/6-benchmarks/#Tips","page":"Benchmarking","text":"Use QUBODrivers.benchmark as a quick interface smoke benchmark for a new sampler wrapper.\nUse ExactSampler on small instances to obtain a known optimum.\nRun multiple independent trials for stochastic samplers and report summary statistics.\nKeep model generation outside the timed region unless model construction is part of the benchmark.\nUse BenchmarkTools.jl for micro-benchmarks with @benchmark or @btime.\nScale problem sizes gradually to understand how sampler performance degrades.","title":"Tips"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/6-benchmarks/#QUBODrivers.benchmark","page":"Benchmarking","text":"benchmark(optimizer::Type{S}; objective_sense=MOI.MIN_SENSE) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\nbenchmark(config!::Function, optimizer::Type{S}; objective_sense=MOI.MIN_SENSE) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\nbenchmark(optimizer::Type{S}, source::MOI.ModelLike) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\nbenchmark(config!::Function, optimizer::Type{S}, source::MOI.ModelLike) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\n\nRun a QUBO benchmark problem with a sampler implementation.\n\nWhen source is provided, benchmark the sampler on that MOI model. The no-source methods use a small built-in QUBO instance as a smoke benchmark.\n\nThe optional config! function receives the raw sampler optimizer after the source model is copied and before optimization. This is useful for setting sampler attributes such as seeds, number of reads, time limits, or warm-starts.\n\nThe return value is a named tuple with solver metadata, benchmark problem size, result count, objective values, read counts, objective-value summary statistics, the best result, MOI solve time, measured wall time around MOI.optimize!, and solver status fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.benchmark"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#Metadata-Schema","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"Sampler metadata is the contract between a driver and benchmarking or conformance tooling. Every SampleSet returned through MOI.optimize! should carry the fields below. QUBODrivers validates this schema after it stamps framework metadata and emits a warning, not an error, for violations.\n\nUse QUBODrivers.validate_metadata(sampleset) to check a result directly. The function returns a vector of human-readable violations; an empty vector means the metadata satisfies the current schema.","title":"Metadata Schema"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#Required-Fields","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"Field Type Required Stamped by Meaning\nmetadata[\"origin\"] String yes driver Human-readable driver or backend origin.\nmetadata[\"algorithm\"][\"name\"] String yes driver Algorithm or sampler name used for grouping benchmark rows.\nmetadata[\"backend\"][\"name\"] String yes driver Backend package, service, or solver name.\nmetadata[\"backend\"][\"version\"] VersionNumber, String, or nothing yes driver Backend version when known. Use nothing only when the backend cannot report one.\nmetadata[\"status\"] String yes driver or QUBODrivers fallback Raw status string suitable for logs and reports.\nmetadata[\"reads\"][\"number_of_reads\"] non-negative Integer yes driver Reads, evaluations, or backend samples actually consumed by the sampler.\nmetadata[\"reads\"][\"final_number_of_reads\"] non-negative Integer yes driver Number of reads represented in the final emitted SampleSet.\nmetadata[\"seeds\"] dictionary with string keys yes driver and QUBODrivers seed recorder Seed values used by the sampler, backend, model generator, or optimizer. Empty is valid for deterministic solvers.\nmetadata[\"time\"][\"total\"] non-negative Real yes QUBODrivers Wall-clock seconds around sample, post-sample callbacks, and attachment preparation.\nmetadata[\"time\"][\"effective\"] non-negative Real yes driver Backend solve or sampling seconds, excluding framework overhead when the backend exposes that split.\n\nDrivers may include additional metadata fields for backend diagnostics. Prefer string keys and stable, machine-readable values.","title":"Required Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#Timing","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"metadata[\"time\"] has two standardized entries:\n\n\"total\" is QUBODrivers wall-clock time. It is always stamped by the framework when MOI.optimize! attaches a result.\n\"effective\" is backend solve or sampling time. The driver must stamp it.\n\nAdditional timing fields are optional. Use concise names such as \"build\", \"queue\", or \"decode\" when their meaning is clear for the backend, or a nested dictionary when a driver needs a namespaced breakdown.\n\nMOI.get(sampler, MOI.SolveTimeSec()) returns QUBODrivers.effective_time(sampler), not total wall-clock time. Use QUBODrivers.total_time(sampler) or QUBODrivers.total_time(sampleset) when a benchmark needs framework-inclusive wall time.","title":"Timing"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#Seeds","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"Generated optimizers opt into the standard seed contract by declaring raw \"seed\" in their @setup attributes:\n\nQUBODrivers.@setup Optimizer begin\n    name = \"Example Sampler\"\n    attributes = begin\n        \"seed\"::Union{Integer,Nothing} = nothing\n    end\nend\n\nUsers can then call MOI.set(sampler, QUBODrivers.RandomSeed(), 123) or set the raw optimizer attribute \"seed\". When a non-nothing seed is configured, QUBODrivers records it as metadata[\"seeds\"][\"sampler\"] after sampling.\n\nUse QUBODrivers.supports_seed(sampler) to decide whether a benchmark harness can set QUBODrivers.RandomSeed() without driver-specific special cases.","title":"Seeds"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#Capability-Traits","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"QUBODrivers exposes conservative trait functions for benchmark harnesses:\n\nQUBODrivers.supports_seed(sampler) reports support for QUBODrivers.RandomSeed().\nQUBODrivers.honors_final_reads(sampler) reports whether QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads() controls the final emitted read count.\nQUBODrivers.enforces_time_limit(sampler) reports whether MOI.TimeLimitSec() is enforced by the sampler contract.\nQUBODrivers.supports_auto_configure(sampler) reports whether QUBODrivers.AutoConfigure() scales solver parameters automatically from problem size n (preserving explicit user attribute overrides and logging chosen values in metadata[\"auto_configure\"]).\n\nThe default for each trait is false. Drivers should define a method returning true only when the behavior is part of their public contract. Forwarding a time limit to a backend is not the same as guaranteeing enforcement unless the driver can rely on that backend behavior.","title":"Capability Traits"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#Composite-accounting","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"The existing schema permits additional dictionaries with string keys. A composite should describe its counters and timing scopes in a package-specific namespace, without changing the meaning of other drivers' metadata.\n\nFor example, QUBODecomposition's accounting contract uses a \"decomposition\" dictionary with schema version 1:\n\nQuantity Meaning in this composite\nreads.number_of_reads Independently evaluated full candidates, including the initial state and lifted child rows; duplicate evaluations still consume work.\nreads.final_number_of_reads Emitted multiplicity: one for a validated global incumbent, zero if none exists.\ndecomposition.candidate_evaluations Parent evaluation-budget consumption, separate from attempted/completed child-call counters.\ndecomposition.calls[k].reported_results Number of rows reported by that child, when queried successfully; a complete scan is separately validated.\ndecomposition.calls[k].reported_multiplicities Child row multiplicities when available through public QUBOTools queries; these are diagnostics, not parent multiplicities.\ndecomposition.calls[k].physical_reads nothing when physical observations are unknown. Enumeration and constructed states do not establish hardware measurements.\n\nDo not add or multiply child multiplicities to imply independently observed global samples. A composite may construct a single global state from several child solves; that state has no physical global-read count merely because each child reported reads. Keep unknown observations in optional diagnostics, rather than putting nothing in either required integer read field. This convention does not redefine the read counters of ordinary backend drivers.\n\nFor this composite, time.effective includes parent preparation, conditioning, copying/configuration, child execution, validation/reconstruction and independent original-energy evaluation. MOI.SolveTimeSec() returns that effective time. Leave time.total unset in sample so the framework fills the enclosing measurement, including post-sample callbacks. The current framework fills a missing total; it preserves a total already supplied by a driver.\n\nThe package's decomposition.phase_sec breakdown is disjoint; its sum is at most effective time, which also includes unclassified orchestration and final sample preparation. child_execution_sec aliases the execution phase and must not be added again. Parent effective time already contains child execution: neither child-reported effective times nor child wall times should be added to the parent total. Keep per-call diagnostics separate and state their scope.\n\nA parent limit covers one invocation, including its preparation and reconstruction. A child limit covers one child call; the forwarded limit can be the minimum of its factory-configured limit, per-child cap and remaining parent time. An outer ToQUBO refinement budget covers repeated compiler/composite invocations and is another scope. Automatic refinement in released ToQUBO 0.7.1 does not establish a shared wall-clock deadline. Forwarding MOI.TimeLimitSec cannot forcibly cancel an opaque synchronous child, so this composite reports enforces_time_limit == false and records overruns.\n\nLikewise, supports_seed == true describes the parent's public seed support; record each derived child seed and whether the child accepts it. Repeatability also depends on child behavior, ordered input, versions and effective work; wall-clock caps and arbitrary factories cannot guarantee it. This composite accepts FinalNumberOfReads but reports honors_final_reads == false. See its results contract and Public composition pattern for conservative result/status handling.","title":"Composite accounting"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#Checking-metadata","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"violations = QUBODrivers.validate_metadata(sampleset)\nisempty(violations) || @warn \"metadata is not benchmark-ready\" violations\n\nMOI.optimize! runs the same validation after framework metadata is stamped. Violations are warnings so existing drivers have a migration path, but conformance tests can choose to fail on any non-empty violation list.","title":"Checking metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/metadata/#API","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.RandomSeed","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"RandomSeed()\n\nGeneric sampler optimizer attribute for a reproducibility seed.\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"seed\", and the default value is nothing. Generated optimizers support this typed attribute when the driver declares the raw \"seed\" attribute in its QUBODrivers.@setup block. When a seed is set, QUBODrivers records it under metadata[\"seeds\"][\"sampler\"] in the emitted SampleSet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.RandomSeed"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.random_seed","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"random_seed(sampler)\n\nReturn the configured sampler seed, or nothing when the sampler does not support the standard seed attribute or no seed is configured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.random_seed"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.total_time","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"total_time(sampleset_or_sampler)\n\nReturn metadata[\"time\"][\"total\"], the wall-clock seconds measured around the QUBODrivers sampling pipeline, or nothing when it is not available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.total_time"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.effective_time","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"effective_time(sampleset_or_sampler)\n\nReturn metadata[\"time\"][\"effective\"], the backend solve/sampling seconds reported by the driver, or nothing when it is not available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.effective_time"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.validate_metadata","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"validate_metadata(sampleset::SampleSet)\nvalidate_metadata(metadata::AbstractDict)\n\nReturn a list of benchmarking metadata schema violations.\n\nThe validator is intentionally non-throwing so driver authors can use it while migrating metadata. MOI.optimize! calls it after QUBODrivers stamps framework metadata and emits a warning when the returned list is not empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.validate_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.supports_seed","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"supports_seed(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler advertises support for the standard RandomSeed attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.supports_seed"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.honors_final_reads","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"honors_final_reads(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler uses FinalNumberOfReads to control the number of samples emitted in the final SampleSet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.honors_final_reads"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.enforces_time_limit","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"enforces_time_limit(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler enforces MOI.TimeLimitSec() as part of its own sampling contract. The default is conservative because many wrappers only store or forward the attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.enforces_time_limit"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.AutoConfigure","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"AutoConfigure()\n\nGeneric opt-in sampler optimizer attribute enabling problem-size-dependent parameter scaling (n = QUBOTools.dimension(sampler)).\n\nThe raw optimizer attribute key is \"auto_configure\", and the default value is false so existing solver behavior is unchanged unless explicitly enabled. Samplers that implement size-based scaling advertise support via supports_auto_configure, respect any explicitly user-set solver attributes over auto-scaled values (verifiable via has_explicit_raw_attr), clamp scaled parameters to explicit finite bounds, and log the chosen scaling details under metadata[\"auto_configure\"].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.AutoConfigure"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.auto_configure","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"auto_configure(sampler)::Bool\n\nReturn whether automatic problem-size parameter scaling (AutoConfigure) is enabled on sampler. Samplers that do not support the attribute default to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.auto_configure"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.supports_auto_configure","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"supports_auto_configure(sampler_or_type)::Bool\n\nReturn whether a sampler implements automatic problem-size parameter scaling when AutoConfigure is set to true. Defaults to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.supports_auto_configure"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/metadata/#QUBODrivers.has_explicit_raw_attr","page":"Metadata Schema","text":"has_explicit_raw_attr(sampler, key::Union{Symbol,AbstractString})::Bool\n\nReturn true when the raw optimizer attribute key has been explicitly set by the caller on sampler (with a non-nothing value), and false when the attribute is still at its default value. Drivers implementing AutoConfigure use this check to preserve user override precedence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.has_explicit_raw_attr"},{"category":"section","location":"#QUBODrivers.jl-Documentation","page":"Home","text":"","title":"QUBODrivers.jl Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Introduction","page":"Home","text":"QUBODrivers.jl provides a common MathOptInterface-compatible API for QUBO samplers and annealing backends.\n\nThe package includes:\n\nutility samplers for exact enumeration, random sampling, warm-start identity sampling, and MIP-backed baseline solving;\na macro for declaring sampler optimizers and user-facing attributes;\nMOI and QUBOTools plumbing for model conversion and result access;\na reusable test suite for sampler implementations.\n\nIf you want to solve a model, start with Solving QUBO. If you want to implement a new sampler wrapper, start with Sampler Setup and Implementing a Sampler.","title":"Introduction"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"QUBODrivers.jl is registered in Julia's General Registry and is available for download using the standard package manager.\n\njulia> import Pkg\n\njulia> Pkg.add(\"QUBODrivers\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example","page":"Home","text":"using JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(ExactSampler.Optimizer)\n\nQ = [\n    -1.0  2.0  2.0\n     2.0 -1.0  2.0\n     2.0  2.0 -1.0\n]\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, x' * Q * x)\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nfor i = 1:result_count(model)\n    xi = value.(x; result=i)\n    yi = objective_value(model; result=i)\n\n    println(\"f($xi) = $yi\")\nend\nf([1.0, 0.0, 0.0]) = -1.0\nf([0.0, 1.0, 0.0]) = -1.0\nf([0.0, 0.0, 1.0]) = -1.0\nf([0.0, 0.0, 0.0]) = 0.0\nf([1.0, 0.0, 1.0]) = 2.0\nf([0.0, 1.0, 1.0]) = 2.0\nf([1.0, 1.0, 0.0]) = 2.0\nf([1.0, 1.0, 1.0]) = 9.0","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing-QUBODrivers.jl","page":"Home","text":"<!– citation-policy:start –> For general use of QUBODrivers.jl and the broader QUBO.jl ecosystem, cite the published journal article. The earlier arXiv preprint remains available, but the journal article is the version of record.\n\nFor a direct software citation, use the Zenodo concept DOI. The concept DOI is the evergreen identifier for QUBODrivers.jl, resolves to the latest archived release, and is the DOI recorded in CITATION.cff. When reproducibility requires an exact release, cite the matching Zenodo version DOI instead; the archive for v0.6.6 is 10.5281/zenodo.23420133.\n\nThe BibTeX entries below cite the ecosystem article and the v0.6.6 software archive. Replace the @software entry's doi and url with the concept DOI when an evergreen software citation is preferred:\n\n@article{xavier2026qubojl,\n  author       = {Maciel Xavier, Pedro and Ripper, Pedro and Andrade, Tiago and Dias Garcia, Joaquim and Maculan, Nelson and Bernal Neira, David E.},\n  title        = {{QUBO.jl: a Julia ecosystem for quadratic unconstrained binary optimization}},\n  journal      = {Optimization Methods and Software},\n  year         = {2026},\n  pages        = {1--24},\n  doi          = {10.1080/10556788.2026.2702926},\n  url          = {https://doi.org/10.1080/10556788.2026.2702926}\n}\n\n@software{qubodrivers:2026,\n  author       = {Maciel Xavier, Pedro and Ripper, Pedro and Andrade, Tiago and Dias Garcia, Joaquim and Bernal Neira, David E.},\n  title        = {{QUBODrivers.jl}},\n  year         = {2026},\n  publisher    = {Zenodo},\n  version      = {v0.6.6},\n  doi          = {10.5281/zenodo.23420133},\n  url          = {https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23420133},\n  note         = {Evergreen concept DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.7811951}\n}\n\nMachine-readable metadata is in CITATION.cff, with BibTeX in CITATION.bib. <!– citation-policy:end –>","title":"Citing QUBODrivers.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/1-intro/#QUBODrivers.jl-Booklet","page":"Introduction","text":"This booklet aims to provide an advanced overview of the QUBODrivers.jl package, delving into the details of the package's inner workings. It is meant to discuss the package's design choices and provide a guide for developers who wish not only to implement new interfaces but also to extend the package's functionality. Reading this booklet is not required to use the package, but it is strongly recommended for those who wish to contribute to the project.","title":"QUBODrivers.jl Booklet"},{"category":"section","location":"booklet/1-intro/#Table-of-Contents","page":"Introduction","text":"Pages = [\"2-interface.md\", \"3-attributes.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Table of Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/5-tests/#Test-Suite","page":"Test Suite","text":"QUBODrivers includes a reusable test suite for sampler authors. It checks the MOI interface, model conversion, result queries, fixed variables, and example QUBO/Ising problems.\n\nQUBODrivers.test is the public entry point. Its methods are provided by the QUBODrivers_Test_Ext package extension, which Julia loads automatically when both QUBODrivers and Test are present in the active environment. This keeps Test out of QUBODrivers' hard dependencies while still exposing a stable testing API.\n\nThe extension module names themselves, such as QUBODrivers_Test_Ext and MOI_PythonCall_Ext, are internal implementation details. Users should call the public APIs they enable, like QUBODrivers.test, instead of importing those extension modules directly.","title":"Test Suite"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/5-tests/#Basic-Usage","page":"Test Suite","text":"Add Test to the active test environment, then call QUBODrivers.test inside a regular @testset.\n\nusing Test\nusing QUBODrivers\n\n@testset \"My sampler\" begin\n    QUBODrivers.test(MySampler.Optimizer)\nend\n\nIf your sampler needs deterministic configuration, pass a setup function. The function receives each optimizer instance before it is tested.\n\nQUBODrivers.test(MySampler.Optimizer) do model\n    MOI.set(model, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"seed\"), 1)\n    MOI.set(model, MOI.RawOptimizerAttribute(\"num_reads\"), 100)\nend\n\nFor slow backends, disable the example problem set and run only the interface checks:\n\nQUBODrivers.test(MySampler.Optimizer; examples = false)\n\nBenchmark conformance checks are also enabled by default. They exercise a tiny three-variable model and verify that benchmark-facing metadata, timing, reads, seed, time-limit, and termination-status behavior matches QUBODrivers' public schema and traits.\n\nDisable the full conformance group for drivers that are not benchmark-ready yet:\n\nQUBODrivers.test(MySampler.Optimizer; benchmark_conformance = false)\n\nYou can also disable individual groups:\n\nQUBODrivers.test(\n    MySampler.Optimizer;\n    metadata_conformance = false,\n    timing_sanity = false,\n    determinism = false,\n    reads_semantics = false,\n    time_limit_acceptance = false,\n    termination_status = false,\n)","title":"Basic Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/5-tests/#What-the-Suite-Expects","page":"Test Suite","text":"A sampler should:\n\nbe instantiable as an MOI.AbstractOptimizer;\nsupport binary and spin variable domains through MOI;\naccept linear, quadratic, and single-variable objectives;\nhandle fixed variables consistently;\nreturn at least one result for solvable test models;\nexpose result count, objective values, variable primals, solve time, and status through standard MOI attributes.\nreturn benchmark-ready metadata accepted by QUBODrivers.validate_metadata;\nstamp positive metadata[\"time\"][\"total\"] and metadata[\"time\"][\"effective\"], with effective time no larger than total time;\naccept QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads() and either honor it for the final emitted SampleSet length or report honors_final_reads(optimizer) == false;\naccept MOI.TimeLimitSec() without error, and keep total time within a generous smoke-test slack if enforces_time_limit(optimizer) is true;\nreturn a termination status in OPTIMAL, LOCALLY_SOLVED, TIME_LIMIT, ITERATION_LIMIT, or OTHER_LIMIT;\nproduce identical sample states and values for repeated same-seed runs when supports_seed(optimizer) is true.\n\nThe benchmark conformance assertions are intentionally limited to the metadata schema documented on the Metadata Schema page and the public capability traits, including QUBODrivers.validate_metadata, QUBODrivers.supports_seed, QUBODrivers.honors_final_reads, and QUBODrivers.enforces_time_limit.","title":"What the Suite Expects"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/5-tests/#Composite-conformance-evidence","page":"Test Suite","text":"Run every default group through the public entry point for each supported child/configuration path. A successful tiny conformance model checks the driver contract; it does not establish all decomposition algorithms or failure cases. For a composite, supplement it with independent objective/reconstruction oracles, controlled failed children and repeated solves with changed model data.\n\nQUBODecomposition revision 7f7ea818fad9af229c3d871efdc3e1e679953aeb provides concrete evidence using existing released interfaces. Its test/conformance.jl runs QUBODrivers.test(config!, QUBODecomposition.Optimizer) for both a controlled public exact child and released ExactSampler, each at budgets 32 (fitting) and 2 (serial). Test is loaded by test/runtime_floor.jl to activate the extension; no group is disabled and no internal extension module is called. Interface, fixed-variable and example tests run alongside metadata, timing, seed determinism, reads, time-limit acceptance and termination checks. Passing time-limit acceptance does not prove a hard deadline.\n\nContract surface Additional downstream regression evidence at that revision\nComplete states, original objectives, empty/failed/malformed results, duplicate rows and public statuses test/unit/results.jl\nCandidate/call caps, zero budgets, child limits, overruns and seed support test/unit/budgets.jl\nChanged labels, domain, sense, scale, offset and coefficients; stale-result invalidation test/unit/repeated_solves.jl\nComponents, conditioned sweeps, transactional scans, changed graph/plans, multiplicities, disjoint timing and a coupled heuristic gap test/unit/serial.jl\n\nThe downstream acceptance manual maps the broader integration matrix and remaining release/fresh-install work. Its CI workflow at the audited revision runs Julia 1.10/current on Linux and current Julia on Windows, plus released ToQUBO 0.7.1 and dependency-floor lanes. The floor lane pins QUBODrivers 0.6.5, QUBOTools 0.16.2 and MOI 1.0.0 and runs the entire runtime/conformance suite; JuMP integration runs in compatible lanes. Consult exact-revision CI results and resolved versions when reusing this evidence.\n\nThis consumer needs no new upstream public hook or runtime dependency. Its algorithms, registration/release and ecosystem aggregation remain owned by their respective packages. Discover it in External Sampler Packages and use Composite accounting for metadata interpretation.","title":"Composite conformance evidence"},{"category":"function","location":"manual/5-tests/#QUBODrivers.test","page":"Test Suite","text":"test(optimizer::Type{S}; examples::Bool=true, kwargs...) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\ntest(config!::Function, optimizer::Type{S}; examples::Bool=true, kwargs...) where {S<:AbstractSampler}\n\nRun QUBODrivers' interface and example test suite for a sampler implementation.\n\nThese methods are implemented by the QUBODrivers_Test_Ext package extension and become available when Test is loaded in the active environment. The core package does not depend on Test directly.\n\nBenchmark conformance checks are enabled by default. Pass benchmark_conformance=false to disable all benchmark conformance checks, or disable individual groups with metadata_conformance=false, timing_sanity=false, determinism=false, reads_semantics=false, time_limit_acceptance=false, or termination_status=false. The default determinism=:auto runs seed determinism only for samplers whose supports_seed trait is true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.test"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#Samplers","page":"Samplers","text":"QUBODrivers includes four utility samplers. They are intentionally simple: their main role is to exercise the interface, provide small-instance baselines, and make examples runnable without external services or fixed solver choices.\n\nSampler Purpose Main result behavior\nExactSampler.Optimizer Exhaustive enumeration for small models returns every state\nRandomSampler.Optimizer Random baseline and smoke tests returns num_reads random states\nIdentitySampler.Optimizer Warm-start and conversion checks returns the provided start state\nMIPSampler.Optimizer Exact MIP-backed baseline using a user-supplied MOI optimizer returns at most one best incumbent and preserves MIP objective bounds","title":"Samplers"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#Exact-Sampler","page":"Samplers","text":"Use ExactSampler to validate small problem formulations or compare another sampler against a known complete set of states. The cost grows exponentially with the number of variables.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(ExactSampler.Optimizer)\n\n@variable(model, x[1:2], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, -x[1] - 2x[2] + 3x[1] * x[2])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nresult_count(model)\n4","title":"Exact Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#Random-Sampler","page":"Samplers","text":"Use RandomSampler as a cheap stochastic baseline. Set \"num_reads\" to control how many states are sampled and \"seed\" for reproducible examples.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nmodel = Model(RandomSampler.Optimizer)\nset_optimizer_attribute(model, \"num_reads\", 4)\nset_optimizer_attribute(model, \"seed\", 1)\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, x[1] + x[2] - x[3])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nresult_count(model)\n3","title":"Random Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#Identity-Sampler","page":"Samplers","text":"Use IdentitySampler when the requested result is the warm-start vector itself. It is useful in tests for objective evaluation, fixed variable handling, and model conversion.\n\nusing QUBODrivers\n\nMOI = QUBODrivers.MOI\n\nmodel = MOI.instantiate(IdentitySampler.Optimizer; with_bridge_type = Float64)\nx, _ = MOI.add_constrained_variables(model, fill(MOI.ZeroOne(), 3))\n\nfor (xi, start) in zip(x, [1.0, 0.0, 1.0])\n    MOI.set(model, MOI.VariablePrimalStart(), xi, start)\nend\n\nobjective = MOI.ScalarAffineFunction{Float64}(\n    MOI.ScalarAffineTerm{Float64}[\n        MOI.ScalarAffineTerm{Float64}(1.0, x[1]),\n        MOI.ScalarAffineTerm{Float64}(2.0, x[2]),\n        MOI.ScalarAffineTerm{Float64}(3.0, x[3]),\n    ],\n    0.0,\n)\n\nMOI.set(model, MOI.ObjectiveSense(), MOI.MIN_SENSE)\nMOI.set(model, MOI.ObjectiveFunction{typeof(objective)}(), objective)\n\nMOI.optimize!(model)\n\n(\n    round.(Int, MOI.get.(model, MOI.VariablePrimal(1), x)),\n    MOI.get(model, MOI.ObjectiveValue(1)),\n)\n([1, 0, 1], 4.0)","title":"Identity Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#MIP-Sampler","page":"Samplers","text":"Use MIPSampler as an exact correctness baseline for small and medium QUBO instances when you want to choose the MIP backend yourself. The sampler is implemented directly against MathOptInterface and does not depend on JuMP or on any concrete MIP solver package.\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\nusing GLPK\n\nmodel = Model(MIPSampler.Optimizer)\nset_attribute(model, MIPSampler.MIPOptimizer(), GLPK.Optimizer)\n\n@variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n@objective(model, Min, x[1] + x[2] - 2x[1] * x[3])\n\noptimize!(model)\n\nvalue.(x)\nobjective_value(model)\nobjective_bound(model)\n\nWhen a time- or node-limited MIP solve stops early, MIPSampler preserves the backend's MOI.TerminationStatus() and MOI.ObjectiveBound() (translated to the caller's original objective sense, variable domain, scaling, constant offset, and fixed-variable conditioning) separately from any incumbent:\n\nInterrupted with an incumbent: result_count(model) == 1 and primal_status(model) == MOI.FEASIBLE_POINT. objective_value(model) returns the rounded binary state's re-evaluated QUBO objective. objective_bound(model) and relative_gap(model) return the backend-reported bound and relative gap when available, or throw MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed if the backend did not report a finite value.\nInterrupted without an incumbent: result_count(model) == 0 and primal_status(model) == MOI.NO_SOLUTION. MIPSampler returns an empty SampleSet (with zero emitted reads and the consumed solve time) rather than throwing, so callers can still inspect termination_status(model), solve_time(model), and objective_bound(model) (when the backend reported a finite bound). Querying objective_value(model) or value.(x) throws MOI.ResultIndexBoundsError, and querying relative_gap(model) throws MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed{MOI.RelativeGap}.\nNumerical or backend failures: Unlike normal limit terminations, invalid numerical backend outputs (such as NaN or unrepresentable bounds, inconsistent bound > incumbent, or out-of-range binary primals) and backend solve errors throw an exception from optimize!(model), clear any prior results (result_count(model) == 0, primal_status(model) == MOI.NO_SOLUTION), and set termination_status(model) to MOI.NUMERICAL_ERROR or MOI.OTHER_ERROR (or the backend's failure status).\n\nusing JuMP\nusing QUBODrivers\nusing QUBODrivers: QUBOTools\nusing GLPK\n\nconst MOI = JuMP.MOI\n\nfunction solve_interrupted_qubo(stop_reason::Integer)\n    model = Model(MIPSampler.Optimizer)\n    set_silent(model)\n    set_attribute(\n        model,\n        MIPSampler.MIPOptimizer(),\n        () -> begin\n            opt = GLPK.Optimizer()\n            GLPK._set_callback(opt, cb_data -> begin\n                if GLPK.glp_ios_reason(cb_data.tree) == stop_reason\n                    GLPK.glp_ios_terminate(cb_data.tree)\n                end\n            end)\n            return opt\n        end,\n    )\n\n    @variable(model, x[1:3], Bin)\n    @objective(\n        model,\n        Min,\n        -1.25 * (x[1] + x[2] + x[3]) + 2.0 * (x[1] * x[2] + x[2] * x[3] + x[1] * x[3]),\n    )\n\n    try\n        optimize!(model)\n    catch err\n        # Numerical validation or backend failure: prior results are cleared,\n        # and `termination_status(model)` reflects `MOI.NUMERICAL_ERROR` or `MOI.OTHER_ERROR`.\n        @warn \"MIP solve failed\" exception = err status = termination_status(model)\n    end\n\n    status = termination_status(model)\n    bound = try\n        objective_bound(model)\n    catch err\n        err isa MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed ? nothing : rethrow()\n    end\n    raw_metadata = QUBOTools.metadata(QUBOTools.solution(unsafe_backend(model)))\n\n    if result_count(model) > 0\n        incumbent_val   = objective_value(model)\n        incumbent_state = round.(Int, value.(x))\n        gap = try\n            relative_gap(model)\n        catch err\n            err isa MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed ? nothing : rethrow()\n        end\n        return (;\n            status,\n            has_incumbent = true,\n            bound,\n            incumbent_val,\n            incumbent_state,\n            gap,\n            solve_time = solve_time(model),\n            bound_metadata = raw_metadata[\"bound\"],\n        )\n    else\n        # Primal-free limit termination (`primal_status(model) == MOI.NO_SOLUTION`) or failed solve:\n        # `objective_value(model)` and `value.(x)` throw `MOI.ResultIndexBoundsError`, and\n        # `relative_gap(model)` throws `MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed`, while `bound` and timing remain available.\n        return (;\n            status,\n            has_incumbent = false,\n            bound,\n            incumbent_val = nothing,\n            incumbent_state = nothing,\n            gap = nothing,\n            solve_time = solve_time(model),\n            bound_metadata = get(raw_metadata, \"bound\", nothing),\n        )\n    end\nend\n\n# 1. Interrupted at root branching before an integer incumbent is found (bound-only result):\nbound_only = solve_interrupted_qubo(GLPK.GLP_IBRANCH)\n# bound_only.status == MOI.INTERRUPTED, bound_only.has_incumbent == false, bound_only.bound == -1.875\n\n# 2. Interrupted when the first integer incumbent is found (incumbent + bound result):\nwith_incumbent = solve_interrupted_qubo(GLPK.GLP_IBINGO)\n# with_incumbent.status == MOI.INTERRUPTED, with_incumbent.has_incumbent == true,\n# with_incumbent.incumbent_val == -1.25, with_incumbent.bound == -1.25\n\nnote: Bound Scope and Numerical Provenance\nBounds and relative gaps exposed by MIPSampler are backend-reported, tolerance-dependent quantities for the supplied QUBO/Ising model (possibly conditioned on fixed variables), not independently verified exact-arithmetic certificates. A bound on a penalized QUBO relaxation is not automatically a bound on an original constrained formulation.\n\nInternally, MIPSampler linearizes binary products with auxiliary variables and the standard Fortet/McCormick inequalities. The reformulation is a baseline route, not a replacement for specialized large-scale QUBO heuristics. For a broader discussion of efficient binary polynomial reformulations, see Elloumi, Sourour, and Verchere, \"Efficient linear reformulations for binary polynomial optimization problems\", Computers & Operations Research 155 (2023), 106240.","title":"MIP Sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#External-Sampler-Packages","page":"Samplers","text":"Several JuliaQUBO packages implement the same sampler interface for external libraries, heuristics, or services. Their source can be useful when building a new wrapper.\n\nThis table is the reviewed source of truth for public external sampler packages. When a new JuliaQUBO or SECQUOIA driver package is ready for users, open a QUBODrivers documentation PR that adds its package URL, solver type, and source file path.\n\nProject Solver Source Code\nDWave.jl DWave.Optimizer src/sampler.jl\nDWave.jl DWave.Neal.Optimizer src/neal/sampler.jl\nDWave.jl DWave.Tabu.Optimizer src/tabu/sampler.jl\nDWave.jl DWave.Greedy.Optimizer src/greedy/sampler.jl\nDWave.jl DWave.Random.Optimizer src/random/sampler.jl\nQiskitOpt.jl QiskitOpt.QAOA.Optimizer src/QAOA.jl\nQiskitOpt.jl QiskitOpt.VQE.Optimizer src/VQE.jl\nPySA.jl PySA.Optimizer src/PySA.jl\nMQLib.jl MQLib.Optimizer src/MQLib.jl\nQUBODecomposition.jl QUBODecomposition.Optimizer src/optimizer.jl\nCIMOptimizer.jl CIMOptimizer.Optimizer src/CIMOptimizer.jl\nQuantumAnnealingInterface.jl QuantumAnnealingInterface.Optimizer src/QuantumAnnealingInterface.jl","title":"External Sampler Packages"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#QUBODecomposition","page":"Samplers","text":"QUBODecomposition is a standalone composite optimizer configured with a user-supplied child optimizer factory and an explicit free logical-variable budget, max_variables, including isolated variables. It dispatches fitting models whole, solves fitting disconnected components, and uses conditioned serial neighborhood sweeps for oversized connected components under the default :components_then_sweeps strategy. Sweeps return heuristic incumbents; exact child solves do not certify a coupled global optimum. Strict :whole_model and :components strategies reject oversized nonconstant inputs or components.\n\nAs of October 8, 2026, the package is under development, with no published release or General registration verified. Use its canonical development manual for source-based installation, configuration, algorithms and released ToQUBO 0.7.1 integration. See also Public composition pattern, Composite accounting, and Composite conformance evidence. QUBODrivers does not depend on this external package.","title":"QUBODecomposition"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/3-samplers/#Optional-External-Sampler-Designs","page":"Samplers","text":"Before a new provider or hardware-SDK sampler package has a standalone repository, its public driver contract, model/sample mapping, and local conformance test plan are tracked as bounded design notes:\n\nOptional CUDA-Q QAOA Sampler Design (CUDAQOpt.jl design contract, tracked under JuliaQUBO/QUBODrivers.jl#88).\nOptional TKET / Quantinuum Nexus Sampler Design (TKETOpt.jl design contract, tracked under JuliaQUBO/QUBODrivers.jl#89).","title":"Optional External Sampler Designs"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-samplers/#QUBODrivers.ExactSampler.Optimizer","page":"Samplers","text":"ExactSampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nThis sampler performs an exhaustive search over all 2^n possible states. It is useful as a correctness oracle for small QUBO and Ising models.\n\nwarn: Warn\nDue to the exponentially large number of visited states, this sampler is intended only for small instances.\n\nExactSampler ignores QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads; it always returns the full exhaustive sample set. A completed solve exposes MOI.OPTIMAL through MOI.TerminationStatus(). Post-sample transformations do not retain that certificate; annotation-only callbacks preserve it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.ExactSampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-samplers/#QUBODrivers.RandomSampler.Optimizer","page":"Samplers","text":"RandomSampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nSampler that evaluates uniformly random states.\n\nRandomSampler is a lightweight baseline for smoke tests, examples, and benchmark harnesses. It samples independent states in the model domain, evaluates their objective values with QUBOTools, and returns the final sampled states.\n\nAttributes\n\nQUBODrivers.RandomSeed, \"seed\": Random seed to initialize the random number generator.\nNumberOfReads, \"num_reads\": Default final read count.\nQUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads, \"final_num_reads\": Number of random states emitted in the returned sample set. If unset, this defaults to NumberOfReads.\nRandomGenerator, \"rng\": Random Number Generator instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.RandomSampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-samplers/#QUBODrivers.IdentitySampler.Optimizer","page":"Samplers","text":"IdentitySampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nThis sampler selects precisely the state vector provided as warm-start. Use it to check model conversion, objective evaluation, fixed variables, and warm-start plumbing without invoking a stochastic or external backend.\n\nEvery variable must have a valid MOI.VariablePrimalStart value before optimization.\n\nIdentitySampler ignores QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads; it always returns the single warm-start state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.IdentitySampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-samplers/#QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.Optimizer","page":"Samplers","text":"MIPSampler.Optimizer{T}\n\nMOI-native exact baseline that linearizes a QUBO into a binary mixed-integer linear model and solves it with a user-supplied MOI optimizer.\n\nMIPSampler does not depend on any concrete MIP solver. Set MIPOptimizer to a compatible optimizer factory, such as HiGHS.Optimizer, GLPK.Optimizer, or MOI.OptimizerWithAttributes(HiGHS.Optimizer, MOI.Silent() => true).\n\nThe sampler introduces one binary auxiliary variable for each off-diagonal quadratic term and enforces the product relation with the standard Fortet/McCormick inequalities. This follows the usual linear reformulation route for binary polynomial optimization; see also Elloumi, Sourour, and Verchere, \"Efficient linear reformulations for binary polynomial optimization problems\", Computers & Operations Research 155 (2023), 106240.\n\nAt most one best incumbent sample is returned, with its binary state rounded and independently re-evaluated on the full QUBO objective. MIPSampler ignores QUBODrivers.FinalNumberOfReads.\n\nObjective Bounds, Gaps, and Limit Terminations\n\nObjective bounds and gaps (MOI.ObjectiveBound, MOI.RelativeGap): When the MIP backend reports a finite objective bound, MIPSampler translates it back to the caller's original objective sense (MOI.MIN_SENSE or MOI.MAX_SENSE), variable domain (MOI.ZeroOne or QUBODrivers.Spin), scaling, constant offset, and fixed-variable (MOI.EqualTo) conditioning, and exposes it via MOI.ObjectiveBound() as well as metadata[\"objective_bound\"] and metadata[\"bound\"]. If no finite objective bound is available for the current solve state, MOI.get(model, MOI.ObjectiveBound()) throws MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed{MOI.ObjectiveBound}.\nScope: The bound applies to the supplied QUBO/Ising objective, possibly conditioned on any fixed variables in the model. A bound on a penalized QUBO relaxation is not automatically a valid bound on an original constrained problem unless the penalty parameter has been independently certified.\nNumerical provenance: Bounds and relative gaps are backend-reported, tolerance-dependent quantities, not exact-arithmetic certificates. Do not treat them as exact proofs in certified preprocessing without a separate verification layer, and do not infer global optimality from a small observed gap when MOI.TerminationStatus() is not MOI.OPTIMAL. Effective gap tolerances in metadata[\"bound\"] are recorded only when confirmed by backend readback or attribute forwarding; requested sampler settings are stored separately under requested_relative_gap_tolerance and requested_absolute_gap_tolerance.\nRelative gap: When an incumbent and a bound are both present and the backend reports a finite MOI.RelativeGap(), MIPSampler exposes that solver-dependent value through MOI.RelativeGap() and metadata[\"relative_gap\"] (labeled \"backend_solver_dependent\" in metadata[\"bound\"][\"relative_gap_definition\"]). It is not interchangeable with a gap recomputed from the rounded incumbent, and when no finite gap is available (including primal-free results or backends that return NaN/Inf), MOI.get(model, MOI.RelativeGap()) throws MOI.GetAttributeNotAllowed{MOI.RelativeGap}.\nPrimal-free limit terminations vs. failures: If the backend stops on a normal limit or interruption (MOI.TIME_LIMIT, MOI.NODE_LIMIT, MOI.ITERATION_LIMIT, MOI.SOLUTION_LIMIT, MOI.MEMORY_LIMIT, MOI.OBJECTIVE_LIMIT, MOI.NORM_LIMIT, MOI.OTHER_LIMIT, or MOI.INTERRUPTED) without a feasible incumbent, MIPSampler returns an empty SampleSet (MOI.ResultCount() == 0, MOI.PrimalStatus() == MOI.NO_SOLUTION) while preserving MOI.TerminationStatus(), MOI.ObjectiveBound() (when available), and solve timing metadata. Conversely, numerical validation failures (NaN or unrepresentable bounds/objectives/gaps, inconsistent bound > incumbent, invalid primal values) and backend solve errors throw an exception from MOI.optimize!, clear any prior results (MOI.ResultCount() == 0, MOI.PrimalStatus() == MOI.NO_SOLUTION), and set MOI.TerminationStatus() to MOI.NUMERICAL_ERROR or MOI.OTHER_ERROR (or the backend's failure status).\n\nAttributes\n\nMIPOptimizer, \"mip_optimizer\": MOI-compatible optimizer factory used to solve the generated binary MIP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.Optimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"manual/3-samplers/#QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.MIPOptimizer","page":"Samplers","text":"MIPOptimizer()\n\nOptimizer attribute that stores the MOI-compatible MIP backend factory used by MIPSampler.Optimizer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QUBODrivers.MIPSampler.MIPOptimizer"}]
}
